 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059556 1071 "If $k {A} =\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t1 & 3 & 4 \\\n        \t6 & 0 & -2\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack,$\nwhere ${A} =\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t2 & 6 & 8 \\\n        \t12 & 0 & -4\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059556 1071 "If $k {A} = \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {1 & 3 & 4 \\ 6 & 0 & -2} \\right\\rbrack$,\nwhere ${A} = \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {2 & 6 & 8 \\ 12 & 0 & -4} \\right\\rbrack$\nand $k$ is a scalar, calculate the value of $k$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059556 1074 "To express $kA$, we multiply each entry of $A$ by $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nkA &= k \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2k & 6k & 8k \\\\ 12k & 0 & -4k\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kA$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack \\matrix {2k & 6k & 8k \\ 12k & 0 & -4k} \\right\\rbrack = \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {1 & 3 & 4 \\ 6 & 0 & -2} \\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-left elements, $2k$ and $1$. Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2k &= 1 \\\\\nk &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059556 1074 "To express $kA,$ we multiply each entry of $A$ by $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2k & 6k & 8k \\\\ 12k & 0 & -4k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the above result equal to the matrix given for $kA$ in the problem statement:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t2k & 6k & 8k \\\n        \t12k & 0 & -4k\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack\n=\n\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\\matrix {\n        \t1 & 3 & 4 \\\n        \t6 & 0 & -2\n    \t}\n    \t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by equating any two corresponding (nonzero) elements. Let's choose the top-left elements, $2k$ and $1.$ Equating these elements, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k & =1 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We would still get the same result if we chose any other two corresponding (nonzero) elements." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059557 1071 "Calculate $2 {A} + \\dfrac{1}{2} {B}$, where\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and ${B}=\n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        0 & 2 \\\n        4 & 2\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack\n.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059557 1071 "Calculate $2 {A} + \\dfrac{1}{2} {B}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${B}= \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {0 & 2 \\ 4 & 2} \\right\\rbrack$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059557 1074 "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $2$ and each entry of $B$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2A + \\frac{1}{2}B &= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 1 & 2 \\cdot 5 \\\\ 2 \\cdot 1 & 2 \\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 + 0 & 10 + 1 \\\\ 2 + 2 & 4 + 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 11 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059557 1074 "We multiply each entry of $A$ by $2$ and each entry of $B$ by $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and then add the resulting matrices, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A+\\frac{1}{2}B & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0 & 10+1 \\\\ 2+2 & 4+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 11 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059558 1071 "Find $C^T$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059558 1071 "Find $C^T,$ if \n![](images/d5/d5e344ac147321b0440d313274626b1a00527214467cbc85bac199c10fee00bd.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059558 1074 "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n![](images/d6/d64f16633ed91692b0cd22daa4c739a6e4347ec6c0596c992d28088880ea2862.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059558 1074 "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059559 1071 "If\n    ${A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        5 & -1 & 0 \\\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack,$\nthen what is ${A^T} ?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059559 1071 "If\n${A} = \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {5 & -1 & 0 \\} \\right\\rbrack$,\nthen what is ${A^T}$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059559 1074 "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns.\n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{A}^T = \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {5 \\ -1 \\ 0 \\} \\right\\rbrack\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059559 1074 "To find the transpose, we interchange the rows and columns. \n\nHere, there is just a single row, so it becomes a single column:\n\n$$\n{A}^T = \n    \t\t\\left\\lbrack\n    \t\t\\matrix {\n    5 \\ \n    -1 \\ \n    0 \\  \n    \t\t}\n    \t\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059560 1071 "Consider the matrix\n    ${B} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\matrix {\n        1 & 2 & 4 & 2\\\n        4 & 4 & 1 & 1\\\n        3 & -3 & 0 & -1\n    }\n    \\right\\rbrack.$\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^T?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059560 1071 "Consider the matrix\n${B} = \\left\\lbrack \\matrix {1 & 2 & 4 & 2\\ 4 & 4 & 1 & 1\\ 3 & -3 & 0 & -1} \\right\\rbrack$.\nWhat are the dimensions of ${B}^T$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059560 1074 "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $N \\times M$ to $M \\times N$.\n\nSo, since $B$ is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix, the transpose ${B}^T$ is a $4\\times3$ matrix." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059560 1074 "When a matrix is transposed, its dimensions switch from $N \\times M$ to $M \\times N.$\n\nSo, since $B$ is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix, the transpose ${B}^T$ is a $4\\times3$ matrix." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059561 1071 "Find $AB$ and $BA$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059561 1071 "Find $AB$ and $BA,$ if \n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059561 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\cdot 0 + 0 \\cdot (-1) & 1 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\cdot 2 \\\\ -5 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot (-1) & -5 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBA &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot (-5) & 0 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\ -1 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-5) & -1 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that\n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA\n$$\n\nMatrix multiplication is not **commutative**, meaning that when we switch the order of multiplication, we often get a different result (like we did in this example)." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059561 1074 "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-1) & 1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2 \\\\ -5\\cdot 0+1\\cdot (-1) & -5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-5) & 0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\ -1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-5) & -1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that \n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nMatrix multiplication is not **commutative**, meaning that when we switch the order of multiplication, we often get a different result (like we did in this example)." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059562 1071 "Find $AB$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139