end{vmatrix}- 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+ 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 0 - (8 - 12) + 2(-3 - 0) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A$.\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= [\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid \\mid \\mid 1\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}- 3 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid \\mid \\mid 0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid ] \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & - (-4) & -3 \\\\ - (14) & -8 & - (-4) \\\\ 3 & - (-2) & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))C^{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059569 1074 "Let's follow the standard five-step procedure.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the determinant of $A.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & =0|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-(8-12)+2(-3-0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Form the matrix of the minors of $A.$\nTo make this matrix, we replace each entry of $A$ by its minor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 & -3 \\\\ 14 & -8 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Create the matrix of cofactors by changing the sign of some of the entries of $M$ according to the rule of alternating signs.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -(-4) & -3 \\\\ -(14) & -8 & -(-4) \\\\ 3 & -(-2) & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Write down the transpose of the matrix of cofactors.\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Step 5:** Use the formula for the inverse of a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -14 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{2} & 7 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -2 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059570 1071 "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ \nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059570 1071 "The entry $a'_{31}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nequals $1$. Find the value of $k$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059570 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} &= 3\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}- 2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ (-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3(3k - 0) - 2(-6 - 0) - 2(6 + k) \\\\\n&= 9k + 12 - 12 - 2k \\\\\n&= 7k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1}$, we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{13} &= (-1)^{1 + 3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 6 + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{31}^{′} &= (1)/(det(A))c_{13} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7k} \\cdot (6 + k) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 + k}{7k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{6 + k}{7k} &= 1 \\\\\n6 + k &= 7k \\\\\n6 &= 6k \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059570 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k & 0 \\\\ -1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =3|\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3k-0)-2(-6-0)-2(6+k) \\\\  & =9k+12-12-2k \\\\  & =7k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{31}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & k \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7k}\\cdot (6+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{6+k}{7k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that $a'_{31}=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6+k}{7k} & =1 \\\\ 6+k & =7k \\\\ 6 & =6k \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059571 1071 "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059571 1071 "Determine the entry $a'_{23}$ in the inverse of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059571 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &= -2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ (-3)\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-2(2 - 0) - 3(0 - 0) - 3(0 - 1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1}$, we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc_{32} &= (-1)^{3 + 2}M_{32} \\\\\n&= (-1)^{5}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-1 \\cdot (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since\n\n$$\nA^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059571 1074 "First, we need to find the determinant of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(2-0)-3(0-0)-3(0-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry $a'_{23}$ in $A^{-1},$ we need to know the value of $c_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}{M}_{32} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $A^{-1}=\\dfrac{1}{\\det (A)}C^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\na'_{23} = \\dfrac{1}{-1}c_{32} = -1\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059572 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6x+4y=1 \\\\ x-8y-z=-2 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n![](images/ed/ed434344208758284aa0f144676f7dd6498a63faeb0248d18e87c4be6c3d87f2.png)\n\nWhat are the values of $a,$ $b$ and $c?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059572 1071 "The system of equations\n${6x + 4y = 1; x - 8y - z =-2; 3x + 2y - 2z = 1$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & -1 \\\\ 3 & b & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat are the values of $a$, $b$ and $c$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059572 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{6x + 4y + 0z = 1; x - 8y - z =-2; 3x + 2y - 2z = 1\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -8 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=6$, $b=2$, and $c=-2$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059572 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x+4y+0z=1 \\\\ x-8y-z=-2 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/b9/b9364923447e278db5ec47bef74e1a05310645b30df22d4ea5efcc0de46c6ed2.png)\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n![](images/16/16cb1608f70821d138f0c6984038bb42d86d9fbf76d560d94d2e25bd6fdb05e0.png)\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n![](images/0a/0abbbaf34955fa40d6cdbfa9e16cb1567ee16271d65b4565206a0f2204eedf6d.png)\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ becomes\n\n![](images/4b/4b915439c3e9922d692aab4205a728160e0a1286f82fa5ebc736460901581147.png)\n\nTherefore, $a=6,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059573 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z=4 \\\\ z-x+2=-1 \\\\ 2y-2x-4z=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n![](images/c9/c9b7abb2641034c1769c6fe6b13b96edd7ccc282b61424b2031a3d37faa456e4.png)\nWhat are the values of $a,$ $b$ and $c?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059573 1071 "The system of equations\n${2x - y + z = 4; z - x + 2 =-1; 2y - 2x - 4z =-4$\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & a & 1 \\\\ b & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat are the values of $a$, $b$ and $c$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059573 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n{2x - y + z = 4; - x + 0y + z =-3; - 2x + 2y - 4z =-4\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore $a=0$, $b=-2$, $c=-3$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059573 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation in the correct order and all constants are on the right-hand side of the system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z=4 \\\\ -x+0y+z=-3 \\\\ -2x+2y-4z=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/6f/6f1bf319faf8be4a9a79c0a6f9301e6eac48a5b04f66949b679e8445b07e9d9c.png)\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n![](images/fc/fc1ac06787ed64ebefbb1a7a82f9bf785525777f61831bbbc39bd25c7e629d5a.png)\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n![](images/d0/d0249dcecf6b75cd6ff7c4623beac6fd6cfae64a58d043bb6759440167f40ea1.png)\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ becomes\n\n![](images/64/64d1f57717dda57c8a7fbc403728ee9699a22ce995f68a50a0b80f25543fdab6.png)\n\nTherefore $a=0,$ $b=-2,$ $c=-3.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059578 1074 "![](images/bb/bb9f9e2111d71068c98a693977f73ce712c8b7f77cebeebb58db936f72e74fe7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin x$.\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\sin x$.\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $8$ to give $y=-8\\sin x$.\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-8\\sin x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059578 1074 "![](images/bb/bb9f9e2111d71068c98a693977f73ce712c8b7f77cebeebb58db936f72e74fe7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\sin x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $8$ to give $y=-8\\sin x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-8\\sin x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059579 1071 "![](images/37/370671ebb1a5379bc6688a28d886fe83cf9e4a47e3d1e5444df814bad52fde53.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=2\\sin(kx)$. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059579 1071 "![](images/37/370671ebb1a5379bc6688a28d886fe83cf9e4a47e3d1e5444df814bad52fde53.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=2\\sin(kx).$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059579 1074 "![](images/09/097b49a161514a80c99e2b66a025289257bf6877ec2cb25a786c5c68f52ea1b3.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin x$.\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $2$.\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of\n\n$$\ny=2\\sin(-x)\n$$\n\nHence, $k=-1$.\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n2\\sin(-x) = -2\\sin x\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-2\\sin x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059579 1074 "![](images/09/097b49a161514a80c99e2b66a025289257bf6877ec2cb25a786c5c68f52ea1b3.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\sin x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=2\\sin(-x).$ Hence, $k=-1.$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n2\\sin(-x) = -2\\sin x .\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-2\\sin x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059580 1074 "![](images/2f/2f3abbaab4dc2e77ce9a82cd9eb906ef888c331ced45c95547eef03bdd9e721f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\cos x$.\n- Finally, translate it by $2$ units **up** to give $y=-\\cos x+2$.\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\cos x+2\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059580 1074 "![](images/2f/2f3abbaab4dc2e77ce9a82cd9eb906ef888c331ced45c95547eef03bdd9e721f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\cos x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $2$ units **up** to give $y=-\\cos x+2.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\cos x+2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059581 1074 "![](images/5e/5ee965b666188ba4703400135c92c74e6188c8bc882855e84adb80a863d006f7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\cos x$.\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $6$ to give $y=-6\\cos x$.\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-6\\cos x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059581 1074 "![](images/5e/5ee965b666188ba4703400135c92c74e6188c8bc882855e84adb80a863d006f7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\cos x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-\\cos x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $6$ to give $y=-6\\cos x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-6\\cos x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059582 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/14/14671e805c18814b31d0c65d0e53c60aae1cf52c60ad8dd146d8a3f4bc823915.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059582 1071 "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n${x - 2y = 1; 2y =-1$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059582 1074 "First, we organize the system so that every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/e0/e0318c549ab4aa25bf2decb41e45e296406d699706c03aff7ea44724d20edece.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\matrix {\n\t\t1 & -2 \\\n\t\t0 & 2 \n\t}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059582 1074 "First, we organize the system so that every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\nx - 2y = 1; 0x + 2y =-1\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack \\matrix {1 & -2 \\ 0 & 2} \\right\\rbrack\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059583 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/a5/a5abba041a61cad76d43d5bb13d0fd4e873eb2daf2282e8d9828b8ab79fe2ba0.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059583 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n${y + x - 5 - 3z = 0; z = y + 7x; y + 3 - 2z = 1 - 2x$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059583 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/2f/2f9ae37920ec528a700715658900bbbf3ec4c855767bf06f79d044f488a8bbf2.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\matrix {\n\t\t1 & 1 & -3 \\\n\t\t-7 & -1 & 1 \\\n                2 & 1 & -2 \n\t}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/af/af1499610c0c312a1f5c50d99967a004ae6b3042c3a88ca20a86f922b28aaaff.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059583 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\nx + y - 3z = 5; - 7x - y + z = 0; 2x + y - 2z =-2\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack \\matrix {1 & 1 & -3 \\ -7 & -1 & 1 \\ 2 & 1 & -2} \\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -7 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059584 1071 "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n![](images/4d/4d90e81043121495ae8bb7742400b2c9e705cb7d706f553a6ab2aec44b35d017.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059584 1071 "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059584 1074 "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and $y$, then, the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\n5x - 4y =-12; 3x + y = 1\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059584 1074 "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and $y,$ then, the corresponding system of equations is\n![](images/3e/3e7f783023405be3ea79a740998fefac3a6ea909e9903f5ea300a9234ae63003.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059585 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/d8/d89417b7eb2d000bbd81fcf4a89ca7eaa5529fc56d9b41bf6e8bb237d2eb9e16.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059585 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n${\\frac{c}{2} = a; 5 + 3b - a = 4c; d = 1$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059585 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c,d$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/93/93874fa93cfd9ef3df1492bbc35056c2381157740d8ca26b475474faf54ee4d1.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/83/8356d9148bc06d76a5aee41e4a07651c43d94b2496d9542eb8b6e01ee52ea161.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059585 1074 "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c,d$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n$$\n-a + 0b + \\frac{1}{2}c + 0d = 0; - a + 3b - 4c + 0d =-5; 0a + 0b + 0c + d = 1\n$$\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059586 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}$\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2:= R_2 +(-4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059586 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/62/627d12115d61ddfdedf9db08330269de2137a23abceada977d039e90e5b88675.png)\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059586 1074 "The operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row.\n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-4) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 + (-4)(1) & 2 + (-4)(2) & -1 + (-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059586 1074 "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059587 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059587 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/82/82729e1d34174d58f01547b58aa63db28ec68a4e1964ef5cd127a2167ed4a722.png) \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059587 1074 "The operation\n\n$$\nR_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2\n$$\n\nmeans that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}R_{1}↔R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059587 1074 "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059588 1071 "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059588 1071 "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n![](images/56/56fd7e343edf6c26f36718a30f899af6c8eaf0cad5f6b07c90ffa93c19df6346.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059588 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row.\n\nNow, applying the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 1 \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 + 1 \\cdot (-4) & 1 + 1 \\cdot (-2) & 2 + 1 \\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059588 1074 "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059589 1071 "Given that\n![](images/59/59ee8e91d058e347238320a2bd5496630f33534564f67f901d2ddf89000f0108.png) which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059589 1071 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059589 1074 "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0$.\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0$, because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0$.\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1$. If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22}$, we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5) \\cdot R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 + (-5) \\cdot 3 & 5 + (-5) \\cdot 1 & 6 + (-5) \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2 -5 R_1\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059589 1074 "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059590 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059590 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n![](images/67/67d01790cfbda1f20a23c9e22a64714156485851ce56b2f73e9873db6ef4bed8.png)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059590 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=-\\frac{1}{8}R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\nx + 2y = 0; y = 1\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y$. Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1$. Or, in vector form,\n\n$$\n\\left< -2,1 \\right>\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059590 1074 "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n![](images/da/da9c44be043ad3a15808a9e0dd77636697170047e61b73a0d591bc3581d992fb.png)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=1$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2(1)=0 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ Or, in vector form, $\\left<  -2,1 \\right>.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059591 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/a8/a80ecf94bbd4f0ea4b7a860e340039ca80bc5ccd0d2a19503cb9dcc571558976.png)\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059591 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n$-x + 5y = 5; x - 6y = 5$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186059591 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/ab/ab29f353f00454b6e8eeb490336e2b33d0b2cf6cd47f4d24ac6c8a8f042af628.png)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n![](images/ee/ee5b759f55931cf4935a0763f22c9eb5deedb04f3e247c295aa2a1da09b121b4.png)\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y.$ Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \n\t\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n\tx=-55.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186059591 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=-R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=-R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\nx - 5y =-5; y =-10\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $y=-10$ tells us the value of $y$. Substituting this value into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-5(-10)=-5 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=-55\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-55$ and $y=-10$. Or, in vector form, $\\langle -55,-10 \\rangle$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064047 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064047 1071 "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064047 1074 "The function $f(x)$  is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2).$ \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that \n\n$$\nf'(c)   = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c & =2 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064047 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$.\n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(2) - f(0))/(2 - (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} - 0^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) = 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064048 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\,$.*\n\n![](images/56/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064048 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4).$  Complete the sentence below.\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\, .$ *\n\n![](images/56/5672cc68e55078623f34f01650d3e82bd3cee62e6ca97fabcd2287f2a865501a.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064048 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n> *There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n> $f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}$.*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4$, so the above statement becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(4) - f(-1))/(4 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 - 2}{4 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064048 1074 "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b),$ the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n*There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n$f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.$*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4,$ so the above statement becomes \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(-1)}{4-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2}{4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064049 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$?\n\n![](images/e6/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064049 1071 "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[−2,2]?$\n\n![](images/e6/e680c2e80874a4c2e8b4d31fcc7a6ba42a3c55fb7c6f559e42f3cd785b22e3ed.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064049 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$, the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2)$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2$. So it is not continuous on $[-2,2]$, and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$, it is right-continuous at $x=-2$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0$. So, it is not differentiable at $x=0$, which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2)$. Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064049 1074 "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2).$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2.$ So it is not continuous on $[-2,2],$ and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2),$ so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2,$ it is right-continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[−2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0.$ So, it is not differentiable at $x=0,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(−2,2).$  Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064050 1071 "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](images/08/0811362e235ec7c6e93a8bf892a451f8c347d0f633f251b9ed955062a53dd6c3.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064050 1071 "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below?\n\n1. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=2$\n2. $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=2$\n3. $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=2$\n\n![](images/08/0811362e235ec7c6e93a8bf892a451f8c347d0f633f251b9ed955062a53dd6c3.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064050 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0$. In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0$.\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064050 1074 "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where $f'(x) = 0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, $f'(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2$ since it is the highest point of a peak in a graph.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a critical point at $x=2$ since each stationary point is a critical point.\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064051 1071 "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064051 1071 "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$ on the interval $[-1,4]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064051 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 2(-1) - (-1)^{2} =-3 \\\\\nf(1) &= 2(1) - (1)^{2} = 1 \\\\\nf(4) &= 2(4) - (4)^{2} =-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2$.\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1$, so the global maximum value is $1$.\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8$, so the global minimum value is $-8$.\n\n![](images/c2/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064051 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](images/c2/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064052 1071 "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064052 1071 "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064052 1074 "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^4 - 6x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 12x &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x^{2} - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n4x(x + \\sqrt{3})(x - \\sqrt{3}) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73\n$$\n\ndoes not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nand the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(-1) & = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5 \\\\[5pt] f(0) & = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0 \\\\[5pt] f(\\sqrt 3) & = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9 \\\\[5pt] f(3) & = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9$.\n\n![](images/74/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064052 1074 "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](images/74/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064053 1071 "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) =  x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064053 1071 "Find the $x$-values of the stationary points of $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2$ and classify each point." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064053 1074 "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 16 x\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x^{3} - 16x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{3} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx(x + 2)(x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2$.\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x)$, we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-2) &= 12(-2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 16 =-16 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 16 = 32 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0$, so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2$, so these are relative minima." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064053 1074 "First, we need to identify the critical points. Differentiating $f(x) = x^4 - 8 x ^ 2 + 2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 4x^3 - 16 x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-16x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ x(x+2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$\n\nNext, we need to determine the sign of $f''(x)$ at the critical points. Computing $f''(x),$ we get\n\n$$\nf''(x)= 12 x^2 - 16,\n$$\n\nand evaluating $f''(x)$ at each critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}-16=32>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-16=-16<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-16=32>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second derivative is negative at $x=0,$ so this is a relative maximum. On the other hand, the second derivative is positive at $x=\\pm 2,$ so these are relative minima." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064054 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x$. Given that $f'(5) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f''(5) > 0$\n2. $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n3. $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064054 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^3-6x^2-15x.$ Given that $f'(5) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f''(5)  >           0$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=5$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064054 1074 "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x)$. We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^3-6x^2-15x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0$. Indeed, we can see that\n\n$$\nf'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x) &= 6x - 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(5) &= 6(5) - 12 \\\\\n&= 30 - 12 \\\\\n&= 18 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064054 1074 "We're given that $x=5$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=  x^3-6x^2-15x,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) =  3x^2-12x-15\n$$\n\nWe're given that $f'(5) = 0.$ Indeed, we can see that $f'(5) = 3(5)^2 - 12(5) - 15 = 0.$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =6x-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5) & =6(5)-12 \\\\  & =30-12 \\\\  & =18>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064055 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6e^x  \\textrm d x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064055 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6e^x \\textrm d x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064055 1074 "Factoring out the constant $6$ and taking the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int 6e^x \\textrm d x =6 \\int e^x \\, \\textrm d x =6e^x +C. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064055 1074 "Factoring out the constant $6$ and taking the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\int 6e^x   \\textrm d x   =6 \\int e^x  \\, \\textrm d x =6e^x +C.\n                                                                               \n}\n$$" 13194139533