egin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/3}\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos^{2} x}dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/3}\\sec x\\tan xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sec x\\right|_{0}^{π/3} \\\\\n&= \\sec (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\sec (0) \\\\\n&= 2 - 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064101 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064101 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186064101 1074 "We use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}\n$$\n\nand rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}dx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}u & + C \\\\ =-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln & \\cos 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}(\\cos 2x)^{-1} & + C \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln & \\sec 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186064101 1074 "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186068553 1071 "Is the function $y=f(x)$, plotted below, continuous at $x=0$?\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186068553 1071 "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, continuous at $x=0?$\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186068553 1074 "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0$, it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186068553 1074 "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186068554 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x)$, plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186068554 1071 "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186068554 1074 "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0$, $