nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= \\int(\\frac{1}{u})\\frac{du}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid x^{2} + 4 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second integral, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+a^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for the third integral we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx &= \\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int(2x + 3)/((x^{2} + 4)(x - 1))dx &= -\\int\\frac{x}{x^{2} + 4}dx + \\int\\frac{1}{x^{2} + 4}dx + \\int\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln (x^{2} + 4) + \\frac{1}{2}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{2}) + \\ln \\mid x - 1 \\mid + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186068569 1074 "The quadratic factor $x^2+4$ is irreducible, so we proceed with the following partial fraction decomposition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x^2+4)(x-1),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the unknown constants using the usual methods, we get $A=-1, B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{-x+1}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nNow we integrate this function term by term, adding the constant of integration at the very end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{-x+1}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d