nd{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078297 1074 "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\int_{6}^{0}f(x)dx =-\\int_{0}^{6}f(x)dx = 54\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x) + 1)dx &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{6}^{0}f(x)dx + \\int_{6}^{0}1dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 54 + \\left.x\\right|_{6}^{0} \\\\\n&= 18 + (0 - 6) \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078301 1071 "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big( 2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078301 1071 "If $\\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = 18$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = -6$ then what is $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{10} \\big(2f(x)+1 \\big) \\,\\textrm d x$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078301 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n-6 + 18 = \\int_{0}^{10}f(x)dx; 12 = \\int_{0}^{10}f(x)dx\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{10}(2f(x) + 1)dx &= 2\\int_{0}^{10}f(x)dx + \\int_{0}^{10}dx \\\\\n&= 2(12) + \\left.x\\right|_{0}^{10} \\\\\n&= 24 + (10 - 0) \\\\\n&= 34\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078301 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078303 1071 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series:\n\n$$\n5+7+9+\\cdots = 140\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078303 1071 "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series:\n$5+7+9+\\cdots = 140$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078303 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_{N} = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big( 2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe are given the sum $S_N = 140,$ and the first term $a_1=5.$ Our goal is to find $N,$ the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =7-5 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(5)+(N-1)(2)) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(10+2N-2) \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2N+8) \\\\ 140 & ={N}^{2}+4N \\\\ 0 & ={N}^{2}+4N-140.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-140)}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{576}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 24}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=10$ or $N=-14.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078303 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_{N} = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe are given the sum $S_N = 140$, and the first term $a_1=5$. Our goal is to find $N$, the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 7 - 5 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2(5) + (N - 1)(2)) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(10 + 2N - 2) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2N + 8) \\\\\n140 &= N^{2} + 4N \\\\\n0 &= N^{2} + 4N - 140\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &= (-4 ± \\sqrt{4^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-140)})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± \\sqrt{576}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± 24}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=10$ or $N=-14$. We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078304 1071 "An arithmetic series has the $5$th term $a_{5}=14$, the $7$th term $a_{7}=19$, and the sum of the series is $S_N=850$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078304 1071 "An arithmetic series has the $5$th term $a_{5}=14,$ the $7$th term $a_{7}=19,$ and the sum of the series is $S_N=850.$ Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078304 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big( 2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{7}-{a}_{5}}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{7-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a_{5}=14.$ We can compute $a_1$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 14 & ={a}_{1}+4\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 14 & ={a}_{1}+10 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting $a_1=4,$ $d=2.5,$ and $S_N=850$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\ 850 & =\\frac{N}{2}(2(4)+(N-1)(2.5)) \\\\ 850 & =\\frac{N}{2}(8+2.5N-2.5) \\\\ 850\\cdot 2 & =N(5.5+2.5N) \\\\ 1700 & =2.5{N}^{2}+5.5N \\\\ 2.5{N}^{2}+5.5N-1\\,700 & =0 \\\\ 5{N}^{2}+11N-3\\,400 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N & =\\frac{-(11)\\pm \\sqrt{(-11)^{2}-4\\cdot 5\\cdot (-3400)}}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11\\pm \\sqrt{68\\,121}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11\\pm 261}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=25$ or $N=-27.2.$ We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=25$ terms." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078304 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{7} - a_{5}}{7 - 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{19 - 14}{7 - 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a_{5}=14$. We can compute $a_1$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\na_{5} &= a_{1} + (5 - 1) \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n14 &= a_{1} + 4 \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n14 &= a_{1} + 10 \\\\\na_{1} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $a_1=4$, $d=2.5$, and $S_N=850$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n850 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2(4) + (N - 1)(2.5)) \\\\\n850 &= \\frac{N}{2}(8 + 2.5N - 2.5) \\\\\n850 \\cdot 2 &= N(5.5 + 2.5N) \\\\\n1700 &= 2.5N^{2} + 5.5N \\\\\n2.5N^{2} +