c{{x}^{3}}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078319 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078319 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078319 1074 "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t  = -  \\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n \\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0  \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \n\t& \\, \\, = \\, \\, \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right)\\\n\t& \\, \\, = \\, \\, -\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right)\\\n\t&= \\, \\, -\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2 +1}.}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078319 1074 "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t =-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\displaystyle\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_x^0 \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t & \\, \\, = \\, \\, \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(-\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right) \\\\ & \\, \\, = \\, \\, -\\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\left(\\int_0^x \\dfrac{t^3}{t^2 +1} \\,\\textrm d t \\right) \\\\ &= \\, \\, -\\dfrac{x^3}{x^2 +1}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078320 1071 "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078320 1071 "Find $f'(x)$ for $f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_2^{x^2}\\arctan t\\,\\textrm d t.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078320 1074 "To compute $f'(x)$, we first make the substitution $u=x^2$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\int_{2}^{x^{2}}\\arctan tdt \\\\\n&= \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot \\frac{d}{du}\\int_{2}^{u}\\arctan tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous for all $t$, so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{du}{dx} \\cdot \\arctan (u) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\cdot \\arctan (u) \\\\\n&= 2x\\arctan (x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078320 1074 "To compute $f'(x),$  we first make the substitution $u=x^2$  and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{du}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe integrand is continuous for all $t,$ so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078321 1071 "Find $f'(1)$ if  $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t)  \\,\\, \\textrm d t.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186078321 1071 "Find $f'(1)$ if $\\displaystyle f(x) = \\displaystyle \\int_x^{x^2} (t^2 + 2t) \\,\\, \\textrm d t$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078321 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{x}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt &= \\int_{x}^{a}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + \\int_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + \\int_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $x^2$.\n\nThe integrand $t^2 + 2t$ is continuous for all $t$, so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(-\\int_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt + \\int_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{d}{dx}(\\int_{a}^{x}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) + \\frac{d}{dx}(\\int_{a}^{x^{2}}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} + 2x) + \\frac{du}{dx}\\frac{d}{du}(\\int_{a}^{u}(t^{2} + 2t)dt) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x(u^{2} + 2u) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x((x^{2})^{2} + 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x(x^{4} + 2x^{2}) \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - 2x + 2x^{5} + 4x^{3} \\\\\n&= 2x^{5} + 4x^{3} - x^{2} - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=x^2$, as before.\n\nFinally, we can compute $f'(1)$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(1) &= 2(1)^{5} + 4(1)^{3} - (1)^{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= 2 + 4 - 1 - 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186078321 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $x^2.$\n\nThe integrand $t^2 + 2t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{{x}^{2}}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}({t}^{2}+2t)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x({u}^{2}+2u) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x(({x}^{2})^{2}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-2x+2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=x^2,$ as before.\n\nFinally, we can compute $f'(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =2(1)^{5}+4(1)^{3}-(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =2+4-1-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186082975 1071 "What is the period of the function $y = 4\\sec\\left(2x-1\\right)-5$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186082975 1071 "What is the period of the function $y = 4\\sec\\left(2x-1\\right)-5?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186082975 1074 "Given a function of the form\n\n$$\ny = A\\sec(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2$. Therefore, the period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2}=\\pi\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186082975 1074 "Given a function of the form $y = A\\sec(Bx +C) + D,$ the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2.$ Therefore, the period is $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{2}=\\pi.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186082976 1071 "Find an expression for the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = 2\\csc\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)-1.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585