ave no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right) \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup  [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\leq -1, \\qquad \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\ge 2-1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\ge 1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\ge 1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)\\le -2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\le -2-1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3x+5)-1\\le -3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x) \\geq 1, \\qquad f(x) \\leq -3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty, -3] \\cup [1, \\infty).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186082977 1074 "The range of $\\csc x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\csc x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right) \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\leq -1, \\qquad \\csc\\left(3x+5 \\right)\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x)$, we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1$, we get\n$\\csc (3x + 5) \\le - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) \\ge 2; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\ge 2 - 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\ge 1; f(x) \\ge 1$.\n- Multiplying the second interval by $-2$ and subtracting $1$, we get\n$\\csc (3x + 5) \\ge 1; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) \\le - 2; - 2\\csc (3x + 5) - 1 \\le - 2 - 1; - 2\\cs