_{1}(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{4}^{N})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{4}^{N})}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(1-{4}^{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{N}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186083006 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $3$rd term is $18$ and the common ratio is $3$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186083006 1071 "What is the sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series if the $3$rd term is $18$ and the common ratio is $3?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186083006 1074 "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine $a_1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1}r^{n - 1} \\\\\n18 &= a_{1} \\cdot 3^{(3 - 1)} \\\\\n18 &= 9a_{1} \\\\\na_{1} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{a_1 (1-r^N)}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1=2$ and $r=3$. Substituting our values into the formula for $S_N$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= (2(1 - 3^{N}))/(1 - 3) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{2}(1 - 3^{N}) \\\\\n&= 3^{N} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186083006 1074 "We use the formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence to determine $a_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\ 18 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{(3-1)} \\\\ 18 & =9{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series can be determined by the formula\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{a_1 (1-r^N) }{1-r}\t.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $a_1=2$ and $r=3.$ Substituting our values into the formula for $S_N$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{N} & =\\frac{2(1-{3}^{N})}{1-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{2}(1-{3}^{N}) \\\\  & ={3}^{N}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186087483 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x - \\sin^2 x = \\cos 2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting\