egin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -0 \\\\ - (-2) & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAA^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓ \\\\\nA^{-1}A &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186087515 1071 "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186087515 1071 "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186087515 1074 "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(A)=0,$ we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18.\n$$\n\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0,$ we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular." 1319