_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186091988 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\cos x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$ Since $\\cos{x} < 0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/bf/bf1efb64724fb0dfbf60cc14905e63ba867caa0bf0edd888b35fb554af97c856.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186091989 1071 "Find the solutions to the equation $\\cos x =1$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186091989 1071 "Find the solutions to the equation $\\cos x =1$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186091989 1074 "Notice that the solutions to $\\cos x = 1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\cos x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0^\\circ.$ \n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx = n (360^\\circ),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nSince the domain $- 180^\\circ   <    x \\leq 180^\\circ$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x = 0^\\circ$ is the only solution." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186091989 1074 "Notice that the solutions to $\\cos x = 1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\cos x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (1) = 0^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $0^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ\n$$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx = n (360^\\circ)\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nSince the domain\n\n$$\n-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nis one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x = 0^\\circ$ is the only solution." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186091990 1071 "Solve the equation $2\\cos x +\\sqrt 2 =0$ for $-180^\\circ< x \\leq 180^\\circ$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186091990 1071 "Solve the equation $2\\cos x +\\sqrt 2 =0$ for $-180^\\circ< x \\leq 180^\\circ.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186091990 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\cos{x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $135^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 135^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos{x} <    0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f32529ce4132c594d8a886222ff6f1a356eefa95fa4c7e1fd38b9bd4e3932a9.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we subtract $360^\\circ$ from $X \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x = - 135^\\circ , 135^\\circ.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186091990 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\cos{x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos x + \\sqrt{2} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\cos x &= -\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\cos x &= -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) = 135^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $135^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 135^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/5f/5f32529ce4132c594d8a886222ff6f1a356eefa95fa4c7e1fd38b9bd4e3932a9.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 180^{∘} + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we subtract $360^\\circ$ from $X$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= X - 360^{∘} \\\\\n&= 225^{∘} - 360^{∘} \\\\\n&=-135^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx =-135^\\circ, 135^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186091991 1071 "Calculate, in radians to ![](images/2f/2f84eb7bb5c17949d98cacb1f217bbbd99ef39032a3a0a960412d0e94bcba8b6.png) decimal place, the solutions to the equation ![](images/a3/a30afdb1e67c4e8bdf14a424eb530802f6167c39e29075b118a03369940ddadd.png) for ![](images/11/114fd23a8df71f5492fb05044bc09c1e8eae3d171fc652152e4f0eb75b1bf0e5.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186091991 1071 "Calculate, in radians to $1$ decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\sin x = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186091991 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n![](images/e9/e9b49c161c6b0931e7c1820edcbfedcc90007d0e92cd177d0710d59b0294a834.png)\n\nSince ![](images/81/81c3cdbb156b235654eb83f7267b015405369d27c87762362dbdb0c6ce262e86.png) lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ![](images/d4/d47d503422b59adebff3b228486469680ef178c688873ed525befa06f7b8361e.png)\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ![](images/10/10c1cfe8b35a3463d20e16b81cfab0f565f3be5c4ae65a1927eb90ada8e529ad.png) Since ![](images/18/18d107587f8a5f4d68598fd340ada7907c6bbdbd4f9e52ed2f1227b90e5b4006.png) the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ![](images/10/10a856d0baf0a5f88993f4c637fff59f9cc2a55749870f517e63c15b16bdb145.png)\n\n![](images/98/9832f760a9c8de833fac4c90133da27a619026a08dc3a33bab04f454d90aea03.png)\n\nSince the second solution ![](images/b2/b21844eb49ecb33e634b92d2ae4f5d01c9852c57fb66290829dbf3cb9adda502.png) lies in the ![](images/a0/a0d74f902505c50a5059c504f5246bd54d83a40235042951c