\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)' \\\\ &=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0 \\\\ &=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096420 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) &= \\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)'\\\n\t\t\t&=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0\\\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x.\n\n}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096421 1071 "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096421 1071 "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096421 1074 "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e$. By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right) \\\\ &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right) \\\\ &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3} \\\\ &=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096421 1074 "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e.$ By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}.\n\n}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096422 1071 "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096422 1071 "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096422 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nat that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x) \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = 1} &= \\left.(\\frac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\frac{1}{x})\\right|_{x = 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\ln 4} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096422 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right)\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x )\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{1}{xln⁡4}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{ln⁡4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096423 1071 "Differentiate $x\\cos x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096423 1071 "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096423 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\cos x\n$$\n\nSo, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x\\cos x)' &= u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x) \\\\ &= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)' \\\\ & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x) \\\\ & = \\cos x - x \\sin x \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096423 1074 "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096424 1071 "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x$, find $f'(x)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096424 1071 "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096424 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x$. So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (x^{2})^{′}\\ln x + x^{2}(\\ln x)^{′} \\\\\n&= 2x \\cdot \\ln x + x^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\\n&= 2x\\ln x + x \\\\\n&= x(2\\ln x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096424 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096425 1071 "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096425 1071 "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096425 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sin{x}\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= u\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{x} \\cdot \\cos x + \\sin x \\cdot e^{x} \\\\\n&= e^{x}(\\cos x + \\sin x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\ninto the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x = π/2} &= e^{π/2}(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) \\\\\n&= e^{π/2}(0 + 1) \\\\\n&= e^{π/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096425 1074 "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096426 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x} {9x^2 + 5x}$, find $f'(x)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096426 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac{x} {9x^2 + 5x},$ find $f'(x).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096426 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x) = x$ and\n\n$$\nv(x) = 9x^2 + 5x\n$$\n\nrespectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′}(x) &= 1, \\\\\nv^{′}(x) &= 18x + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) & = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{\\left(9x^2+5x \\right)\\cdot 1 -x\\cdot \\left(18x+5 \\right)}{\\left(9x^2+5x\\right)^2} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{\\left(9x^2+5x-18x^2-5x \\right)}{\\left(9x^2+5x\\right)^2} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{9x^2}{\\left(9x^2+5x\\right)^2}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096426 1074 "The numerator and denominator are $u(x) = x$ and $v(x) = 9x^2 + 5x,$ respectively. Their derivatives are given by\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =1, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =18x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) \t& = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x \\right )\\cdot 1 -x\\cdot \\left( 18x+5 \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{\\left( 9x^2+5x-18x^2-5x \\right)}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{9x^2}{\\left( 9x^2+5x\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096427 1071 "If $f(x)=\\dfrac{1+\\sin x}{x}$, find $f'(x)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096427 1071 "If $f(x)=\\dfrac{1+\\sin x}{x},$ find $f'(x).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096427 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nu(x) = 1+\\sin x\n$$\n\nand $v(x) = x$.\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f'(x) & = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{(1+\\sin x)'\\cdot (x) -(1+\\sin x)\\cdot (x)'}{x^2} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{\\cos x \\cdot x- (1+\\sin x)\\cdot 1}{x^2} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{x \\cos x -\\sin x -1}{x^2} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096427 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\dfrac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x) = 1+\\sin x$ and $v(x) = x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nf'(x) & = \\dfrac{u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x)\\cdot v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\n\t\t         \t& = \\dfrac{(1+\\sin x)'\\cdot (x) -(1+\\sin x)\\cdot (x)'}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t        & = \\dfrac{\\cos x \\cdot x- (1+\\sin x)\\cdot 1}{x^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{x \\cos x -\\sin x -1}{x^2}\t\t\t\n}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096428 1071 "Given that $y=\\dfrac{1}{3x+7}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096428 1071 "Given that $y=\\dfrac{1}{3x+7},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096428 1074 "Let $u=1$ and\n\n$$\nv=3x+7\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 0and\\frac{dv}{dx} = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} & = \\dfrac{v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}-u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2} \\\\ & = \\dfrac{\\left(3x+7 \\right) \\cdot 0 -1\\cdot 3}{\\left(3x+7\\right)^2} \\\\ & = -\\dfrac{3}{\\left(3x+7\\right)^2}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096428 1074 "Let $u=1$ and $v=3x+7.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\t& = \\dfrac{ v\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}-u\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\dfrac{ \\left( 3x+7 \\right ) \\cdot 0 -1\\cdot 3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = -\\dfrac{3}{\\left( 3x+7\\right)^2}.\n\t\t}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096429 1071 "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8)$. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](images/54/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096429 1071 "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](images/54/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096429 1074 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096429 1074 "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0$, andthe tangent line exists at this point.\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2$. Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4$, andthe tangent line exists at this point.\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6$, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096430 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x$. The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](images/6b/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096430 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](images/6b/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096430 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x$.\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096430 1074 "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096431 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096431 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096431 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0$. However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22 \\\\ f'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\\ f''(x) & = 6x + 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x+ 18 &= 0 \\\\ 6x &= -18 \\\\ x &= -3. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$. So we pick two points around it, say, $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(-4) &= 6(-4) + 18 =-6 < 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(-2) &= 6(-2) + 18 = 6 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3$, we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096431 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096432 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096432 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096432 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0$. However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= x^{4} - 4x^{3} + 6x^{2} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 4x^{3} - 12x^{2} + 12x \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x^{2} - 24x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x^{2} - 24x + 12 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1$. So we pick two points around it, say,\n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2$,\n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(0) &= 12(0)^{2} - 24(0) + 12 = 12 > 0 \\\\\nf^{″}(2) &= 12(2)^{2} - 24(2) + 12 = 12 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1$, we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096432 1074 "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096433 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096433 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096433 1074 "At first, it may not look like we have two factors. Actually, we do, but one of them is implicit. The trick is to write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\ln{x} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing this trick, we can now use integration by parts. We let\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = \\ln x & ⟹u^{′} = \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ v^{′} = 1 & ⟹v = x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, using the by-parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\ln xdx &= x\\ln x - \\intx \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}dx \\\\\n&= x\\ln x - \\int1dx \\\\\n&= x\\ln x - x + C \\\\\n&= x(\\ln x - 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096433 1074 "At first, it may not look like we have two factors. Actually, we do, but one of them is implicit. The trick is to write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\ln{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\ln{x} \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing this trick, we can now use integration by parts. We let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the by-parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+C \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096434 1071 "The area under the curve $y=4-x^2$ between $x=-1$, $x=2$, and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown below. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\n![](images/b5/b517da6e730ac50341b133a60ae8ab99cfed7b2ce92638e6028bb3fac6fbf9da.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096434 1071 "The area under the curve $y=4-x^2$ between $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis is approximated using a left Riemann sum, as shown below. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?\n\n![](images/b5/b517da6e730ac50341b133a60ae8ab99cfed7b2ce92638e6028bb3fac6fbf9da.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096434 1074 "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2$, so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-(-1)}{3} = \\dfrac 3 3 = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(x_{k})Δx &= ∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(a + kΔx)Δx \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(3 - 1)f(-1 + k \\cdot 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)f(k - 1) \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)(4 - (k - 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)(4 - k^{2} + 2k - 1) \\\\\n&= ∑_(k = 0)^(2)(3 + 2k - k^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096434 1074 "The left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ with $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that there are $n=3$ rectangles. The endpoints indicate that $a=-1$ and $b=2,$ so the step size is\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-(-1)}{3} = \\dfrac 3 3 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the left Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(a+kΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{3-1}f(-1+k\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}f(k-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(4-(k-1)^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(4-{k}^{2}+2k-1) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{2}(3+2k-{k}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096435 1071 "The area under the curve $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{ x}{2}\\right)$ between $x=0$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $3$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096435 1071 "The area under the curve $y=\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)$ between $x=0$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $3$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096435 1074 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_i = a + i\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nNote that here we are using the index $i$ instead of $k$. It doesn't matter which letter we use for the counter.\n\nWe're told that there are $n=3$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3} = \\dfrac{3}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i})Δx &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)f(a + iΔx)Δx \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(3)f(0 + i \\cdot 1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(3)f(i) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(3)\\cos (\\frac{i}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096435 1074 "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n f(x_i)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_i = a + i\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nNote that here we are using the index $i$ instead of $k.$ It doesn't matter which letter we use for the counter.\n\nWe're told that there are $n=3$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3} = \\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(0+i\\cdot 1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}f(i) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{i}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096436 1071 "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13}{(x^2+1)(x+1)} = \\dfrac{9x+B}{x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1}$,\n\nwhat is the value of $B$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096436 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13 }{(x^2+1)(x+1) } = \\dfrac{9x+B}{ x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1} ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096436 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x^{2} - 2x - 13 &= (9x + B)(x + 1) - 2(x^{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(0)^{2} - 2(0) - 13 &= (9 \\cdot (0) + B)(0 + 1) - 2((0)^{2} + 1) \\\\\n- 13 &= B - 2 \\\\\nB &= -11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096436 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-2x-13 & =(9x+B)(x+1)-2({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-2(0)-13 & =(9\\cdot (0)+B)(0+1)-2((0)^{2}+1) \\\\ -13 & =B-2 \\\\ B & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096437 1074 "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nbetween the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096437 1074 "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096438 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4-x\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$, and the upper bound is $b=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{3}(4 - x)dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(4 - (1 + \\frac{2k}{n})) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(3 - \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096438 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096439 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b$, given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096439 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096439 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3$.\n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x_k) = x_{k}^2\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^2\n$$\n\nand $b = 8$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186096439 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186096440 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is 