387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100706 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 1,2 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 3, -3 \\rangle.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100706 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n![](images/f4/f4b59236b774117dfc764389f3525a01c5f4c8429cece16552622f54cfb3d863.png)\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = |{-9}| = 9\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100706 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(M) &= \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (-3) - 3 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&=-9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = \\mid {-9} \\mid = 9\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100707 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 3,-2 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 6, -4 \\rangle$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100707 1071 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\textbf{u}=\\langle 3,-2 \\rangle$ and $\\textbf{v}=\\langle 6, -4 \\rangle.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100707 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n![](images/b2/b25d71fb75afcb5e4f9e8ce137ec69fb1dfda54aa544b1636e11503273527844.png)\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)-6\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = |\\,0\\,| = 0\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100707 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(M) &= \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (-4) - 6 \\cdot (-2) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of $\\det(M)$ gives the area $A$ of the parallelogram:\n\n$$\nA = \\mid \\,0\\, \\mid = 0\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100710 1071 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100710 1071 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100710 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{2} + 1 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\ndu &= 2xdx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}du &= xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^2+1\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{u^{4}}{4}\\right|_{2}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1^{4} - 2^{4}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 16}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u$. When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x$. Same for the upper limit." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100710 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^2+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}{|}_{2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{1}^{4}-{2}^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-16}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u.$ When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x.$ Same for the upper limit." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100711 1071 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100711 1071 "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186100711 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{3} + 2 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\ndu &= 3x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}du &= x^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^3+2\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{3}\\ln (u)\\right|_{1}^{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln (10) - \\ln (1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (10)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186100711 1074 "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & ={x}^{3}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & ={x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^3+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u){|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105569 1071 "Given that $p(x)=9x^4$ and $q(x)=3x^4$, what is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105569 1071 "Given that $p(x)=9x^4$ and $q(x)=3x^4$, what is $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105569 1074 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - ∞)(p(x))/(q(x)) &= lim_(x → - ∞)\\frac{9x^{4}}{3x^{4}} \\\\\n&= lim_(x → - ∞)3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105569 1074 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105570 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(-12x^{12}\\right)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105570 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(-12x^{12}\\right).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105570 1074 "The graph $y=x^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^{12} = \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-12$ causes $y=-12x^{12}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → ∞)(-12x^{12}) =-lim_(x → ∞)(x^{12}) = - ∞\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105570 1074 "The graph $y=x^{12}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^{12} = \\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-12$ causes $y=-12x^{12}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-12{x}^{12})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105571 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105571 1071 "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{x^3}{2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105571 1074 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - ∞)\\frac{x^{3}}{2} &= lim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x^{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)x^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=x^3$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} x^3 = -\\infty\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ndoes not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)x^{3} &= lim_(x → - ∞)x^{3} \\\\\n&= - ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105571 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=x^3$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty } x^3 = -\\infty.\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105572 1071 "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4$. Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B$, find the intervals where $f'(x)>0$.\n\n![](images/1a/1a8208a75b7df62a0c692e08f74509ecf1462e3ab1f8dd7eae85a56272abde48.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105572 1071 "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where $f'(x)>0.$ \n\n![](images/1a/1a8208a75b7df62a0c692e08f74509ecf1462e3ab1f8dd7eae85a56272abde48.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105572 1074 "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B$. Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0$.\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0$.\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & (- ∞, 1) & 1 & (1, 4) & 4 & (4, + ∞) \\\\ f & ↘ & min & ↗ & max & ↘ \\\\ f^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105572 1074 "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,1) & 1 & (1,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105573 1071 "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5$, and classify each point." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105573 1071 "Determine the local maxima and minima for $f(x) = 6x-x^3-5,$ and classify each point." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105573 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points,\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nNext, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points\n\n$$\nx = -2, 0, 2\n$$\n\nand evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(-2) &= 6 - 3(-2)^{2} =-6 \\\\\nf^{′}(0) &= 6 - 3(0)^{2} = 6 \\\\\nf^{′}(2) &= 6 - 3(2)^{2} =-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(\\sqrt{2},\\infty\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $f'$ | $\\color{blue}-$ | $0$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $f$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ | min | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | max | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\sqrt{2}$, the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\sqrt{2}$ is a local minimum.\n- At $x=\\sqrt{2}$, the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at\n\n$$\nx = -\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nand one local maximum at\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt 2\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105573 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 6-3x^2.\n$$\n\nSolving $f'(x) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n3(2-x^2)=0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm \\sqrt 2.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x = -2, 0, 2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =6-3(-2)^{2}=-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6-3(0)^{2}=6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6-3(2)^{2}=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $\\left(-\\infty,-\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\left(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\right)$ | 