3+2x} {x}$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105581 1074 "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{3 + 2x}{x}dx &= \\int(\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{2x}{x})dx \\\\\n&= \\int(\\frac{3}{x} + 2)dx \\\\\n&= 3\\int\\frac{1}{x}dx + 2\\intdx \\\\\n&= 3\\ln \\mid x \\mid + 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C$, we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln \\mid x \\mid$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{3 + 2x}{x}dx &= 3\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x & + 2x + C \\\\ = 3\\ln & x\\end{vmatrix}+ 2x + 3\\ln K \\\\\n&= 3\\ln (K \\mid x \\mid) + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105581 1074 "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105582 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow -\\infty} (44-3y^4-9y^{12}) \\, .$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105582 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{y\\rightarrow -\\infty} (44-3y^4-9y^{12}) \\,$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105582 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(y → - ∞)(44 - 3y^{4}- 9y^{12}) &= lim_(y → - ∞)(-9y^{12}) \\\\\n&=-lim_(y → - ∞)(y^{12}) \\\\\n&= - ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105582 1074 "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }(44-3{y}^{4}-9{y}^{12}) & ={lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }(-9{y}^{12}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{y\\to -\\infty }({y}^{12}) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105585 1071 "The area of ​​the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $24\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}^2.$ Find $AB.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93e732bd04cfda304c2098087548a638d8d869ccf8b1050235e8207825875d3.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105585 1071 "The area of ​​the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $24\\sqrt{2} \\text{cm}^2$. Find $AB$.\n\n![](images/e9/e93e732bd04cfda304c2098087548a638d8d869ccf8b1050235e8207825875d3.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105585 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}bc\\sin{A}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{A}$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c$.\n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= AB, \\\\\nb &= AC = 12cm, \\\\\nm∠A &= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\sin{45^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $c=AB$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot c \\cdot \\sin A \\\\\n24\\sqrt{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 12 \\cdot c \\cdot \\sin 45^{∘} \\\\\n24\\sqrt{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 12 \\cdot c \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\\n24\\sqrt{2} &= 3\\sqrt{2}c \\\\\n24 &= 3c \\\\\nc &= 8cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\nAB = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105585 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}bc\\sin{A},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{A}$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\text{ cm}, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{45^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $c=AB,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ 24\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}} & =3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\ 24 & =3c \\\\ c & =8\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105591 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2, -3, -7 \\rangle$. Find $\\mid \\mathbf{a} \\mid$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105591 1071 "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2, -3, -7 \\rangle$. Find $|\\mathbf{a}|$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105591 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + (-3)^{2} + (-7)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 9 + 49} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{62}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105591 1074 "Using the formula, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-7)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+49} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105592 1071 "Let $A(1,1,2)$ and $B(2,-2,3)$. Find $\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} \\mid$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105592 1071 "Let $A(1,1,2)$ and $B(2,-2,3).$ Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105592 1074 "We have\n\n$$\nOA^(→) = a = i + j + 2k,; OB^(→) = b = 2i - 2j + 3k\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= OB^(→) - OA^(→) \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j + 3k) - (i + j + 2k) \\\\\n&= i - 3j + k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid AB^(→) \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-3)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 9 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105592 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=i+j+2k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=2i-2j+3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-2j+3k)-(i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i-3j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105593 1071 "Let $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105593 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105593 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} + (-5)^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 25 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{42}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a}{\\mid a \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4/\\sqrt{42} \\\\ -5/\\sqrt{42} \\\\ 1/\\sqrt{42}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105593 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+25+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{42}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4/\\sqrt{42} \\\\ -5/\\sqrt{42} \\\\ 1/\\sqrt{42}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105594 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z}}{\\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot (-2))/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{30}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{6} \\right) \\approx 111.9^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105594 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{6} \\right) \\approx 111.9^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105595 1071 "Find $\\angle BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,-5)$, $B(-1,0,-4)$, and $C(5,1,1)$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105595 1071 "Find $\\angle BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,-5)$, $B(-1,0,-4)$, and $C(5,1,1).$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105595 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,-5⟩,\\,b=⟨-1,0,-4⟩,\\,c=⟨5,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\\angle BAC = \\theta.$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105595 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\na = ⟨2,-1,-5⟩, b = ⟨ - 1, 0,-4⟩, c = ⟨5, 1, 1⟩\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote\n\n$$\n\\angle BAC = \\theta\n$$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= b - a \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0,-4⟩ - ⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 1, 1⟩ \\\\\nAC^(→) &= c - a \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 1, 1⟩ - ⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3, 2, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= (AB^(→) \\cdot AC^(→))/(\\mid AB^(→) \\mid \\mid AC^(→) \\mid) \\\\\n&= (-3 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 6)/(\\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2} + 6^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105596 1071 "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,0,-3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-1,2 \\rangle$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105596 1071 "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,0,-3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-1,2 \\rangle.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105596 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&= (0 \\cdot 2 - (-3) \\cdot (-1))i - (4 \\cdot 2 - (-3) \\cdot 5)j + (4 \\cdot (-1) - 0 \\cdot 5)k \\\\\n&=-3i - 23j - 4k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3,-23,-4⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\mid a \\cdot b \\mid \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + (-23)^{2} + (-4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{554}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105596 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-1))i-(4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 5)j+(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-3i-23j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-23,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\,a\\times b\\,| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-23)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{554}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105597 1071 "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$, if $A(1,0,-2)$, $B(-2,0,1)$, $C(1,1,-1)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105597 1071 "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ if $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(-2,0,1),$ $C(1,1,-1).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105597 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= b - a \\\\\n&= (-2i + k) - (i - 2k) \\\\\n&=-3i + 3k \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 0, 3⟩ \\\\\nAC^(→) &= c - a \\\\\n&= (i + j