6} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\mid AB^(→) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2^{2} + 0^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{8} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{△} &= \\frac{1}{2}CH \\cdot AB \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{2}CH \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n3 &= CH \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\nCH &= \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nCH &= \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105598 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =4i+2j-4k \\\\ 