, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)+4(0\\cdot 3-1\\cdot (-1))+2(0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-6+4-4 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV = |-6| = 6.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105603 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle k,2,1 \\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -2,-1,1 \\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,1,2 \\rangle$ are coplanar." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105603 1071 "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle k,2,1 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -2,-1,1 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -1,1,2 \\rangle$ are coplanar." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186105603 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot (b \\cdot c) &= \\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= k \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 2 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+ 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= k(-1 \\cdot 2 - 1 \\cdot 1) - 2(-2 \\cdot 2 - (-1) \\cdot 1) + (-2 \\cdot 1 - (-1) \\cdot (-1)) \\\\\n&=-3k + 6 - 3 \\\\\n&=-3k + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot (b \\cdot c) &= 0 \\\\\n- 3k + 3 &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=1$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186105603 1074 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) 