) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115026 1071 "Find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y} {\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 2x - 3\\cot x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115026 1071 "Find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y} {\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 2x - 3\\cot x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115026 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x - 3\\cot x) \\\\\n&= 2 - 3(-\\csc^{2} x) \\\\\n&= 2 + 3\\csc^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115026 1074 "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3cot⁡x) \\\\  & =2-3(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2+3{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115027 1071 "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = x\\sec x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115027 1071 "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = x\\sec x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115027 1074 "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x\\sec x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(x) \\cdot \\sec x + x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sec x) \\\\\n&= (1) \\cdot \\sec x + x \\cdot (\\sec x\\tan x) \\\\\n&= \\sec x(1 + x\\tan x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115027 1074 "We use the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xsec⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sec⁡x) \\\\  & =(1)\\cdot sec⁡x+x\\cdot (sec⁡xtan⁡x) \\\\  & =sec⁡x(1+xtan⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115028 1071 "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ if $f(x) = \\dfrac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115028 1071 "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ if $f(x) = \\dfrac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115028 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{-2\\cot x}{\\sin x}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{d}{dx}(-2\\cot x) \\cdot \\sin x - (-2\\cot x) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x))/([\\sin x]^{2}) \\\\\n&= (-2(-\\csc^{2} x) \\cdot \\sin x + 2\\cot x \\cdot (\\cos x))/(\\sin^{2} x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\csc x + 2\\cot x\\cos x}{\\sin^{2} x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nin the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= (2\\csc (\\frac{π}{4}) + 2\\cot (\\frac{π}{4})\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}))/(\\sin^{2} (\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\sqrt{2}) + 2 \\cdot (1) \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (3\\sqrt{2})/((\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115028 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{-2cot⁡x}{sin⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x-(-2cot⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)}{{[sin⁡x]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot sin⁡x+2cot⁡x\\cdot (cos⁡x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2csc⁡x+2cot⁡xcos⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{2csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})+2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115035 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115035 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115035 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2$, and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3x - 3} &= 9^{x} \\\\\n3^{3x - 3} &= (3^{2})^{x} \\\\\n3^{3x - 3} &= 3^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 3 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115035 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2,$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x-3} & ={9}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={({3}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={3}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-3 & =2x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115036 1071 "What is the range of $y = 2(4)^x+3$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115036 1071 "What is the range of $y = 2(4)^x+3?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115036 1074 "The range of $4^x$ is\n\n$$\n4^x \\gt 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4^{x} & > 0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} & > 0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} + 3 & > 0 + 3 \\\\ y & > 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y \\in (3,\\infty)$.\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2(4)^x+3\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff0d83a05e89b1e56adefe7c9717232907f0f01954cb777e382349e4346776da.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115036 1074 "The range of $4^x$ is\n\n$$\n4^x \\gt 0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $2$ and then adding $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x} & >0 \\\\ 2(4)^{x}+3 & >0+3 \\\\ y & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y \\in (3,\\infty).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $f(x) = 2(4)^x+3$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ff/ff0d83a05e89b1e56adefe7c9717232907f0f01954cb777e382349e4346776da.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115038 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2} 9 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x .$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115038 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2} 9 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115038 1074 "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-1}^{2}9x^{2}dx &= 9\\int_{-1}^{2}x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= 9 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right|_{-1}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\left.3x^{3}\\right|_{-1}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(2^{3} - (-1)^{3}) \\\\\n&= 3(8 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115038 1074 "Factoring the constant out of the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =3({2}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =3(8-(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115039 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = 3(2)^{x} - 48$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115039 1071 "Find the root of $f(x) = 3(2)^{x} - 48.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115039 1074 "To find the root of $f(x)$, we must solve\n\n$$\nf(x)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n3(2)^{x} - 48 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $48$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2)^{x} - 48 &= 0 \\\\\n3(2)^{x} - 48 + 48 &= 0 + 48 \\\\\n3(2)^{x} &= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2)^{x} &= 48 \\\\\n2^{x} &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $16$ as $2^4$ to get\n$2^x = 2^4$.\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n$x = 4$.\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115039 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2)^{x} - 48 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $48$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{x}-48 & =0 \\\\ 3(2)^{x}-\\cancel{48}+\\cancel{48} & =0+48 \\\\ 3(2)^{x} & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{x} & =48 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $16$ as $2^4$ to get\n\n$$\n2^x = 2^4.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115040 1071 "Suppose that $f(x) = 3(e^{5x}) + 2$. Given that $f(x) \\to a$ as $x \\to -\\infty$, then what is the value of $a$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115040 1071 "Suppose that $f(x) = 3(e^{5x}) + 2.$ Given that $f(x) \\to a$ as $x \\to -\\infty,$ then what is the value of $a?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115040 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$:\n\n![](images/b1/b1e4758058c04e38d7077a22e5a6c0a1ff8ccab1596114bbb2517884261fe17c.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to -\\infty$, we conclude that\n$f(x)\\to 2 \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to -\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, $a=2$.\n\nFinally, we note that the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ is $y=2$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115040 1074 "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/b1/b1e4758058c04e38d7077a22e5a6c0a1ff8ccab1596114bbb2517884261fe17c.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=2$ as $x\\to -\\infty,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2 \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\to -\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=2.$\n\nFinally, we note that the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ is $y=2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115041 1071 "What is the $y$-intercept of $y = -2e^{-2x}+3 ?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115041 1071 "What is the $y$-intercept of $y = -2e^{-2x}+3$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115041 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x = 0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -2e^{-2(0)} + 3 \\\\\n&=-2e^{0} + 3 \\\\\n&=-2(1) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115041 1074 "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x = 0$ into the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2{e}^{-2(0)}+3 \\\\  & =-2{e}^{0}+3 \\\\  & =-2(1)+3 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115045 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/4} (\\sec x \\tan x - \\cos x)\\, \\textrm d x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115045 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/4} (\\sec x \\tan x - \\cos x)\\, \\textrm d x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115045 1074 "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/4}(\\sec x\\tan x - \\cos x)dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\sec x\\tan xdx - \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sec x\\right|_{0}^{π/4} - \\left.\\sin x\\right|_{0}^{π/4} \\\\\n&= (\\sec \\frac{π}{4} - \\sec 0) - (\\sin \\frac{π}{4} - \\sin 0) \\\\\n&= (\\sqrt{2} - 1) - (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} - \\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115045 1074 "Splitting up the integral into the difference of two integrals and evaluating each integral separately, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sec}⁡0)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{2}-1)-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115047 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2(2x^3 - 5)\\, \\textrm d x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115047 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2(2x^3 - 5)\\, \\textrm d x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115047 1074 "Splitting up the sum and factoring out the constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{-2}^{2}(2x^{3} - 5)dx &= 2\\int_{-2}^{2}x^{3}dx - 5\\int_{-2}^{2}1dx \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\left.\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right|_{-2}^{2} - \\left.5x\\right|_{-2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{2}x^{4}\\right|_{-2}^{2} - \\left.5x\\right|_{-2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2^{4} - (-2)^{4}) - 5(2 - (-2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(16 - 16) - 5 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115047 1074 "Splitting up the sum and factoring out the constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-5)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2}-5x{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-5x\\,{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{4}-(-2)^{4})-5(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(16-16)-5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115053 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4} \\left(\\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2}\\right) \\, \\textrm d x .$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115053 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4} \\left(\\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2}\\right) \\, \\textrm d x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115053 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_1^4 \\left( \\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_1^4 \\left( x^{1/2}-2x^{-2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\sqrt{x}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+2{x}^{-1}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2})+2({4}^{-1}-{1}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(8-1)+2(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}+2\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115053 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_1^4 \\left(\\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx = \\int_1^4 \\left(x^{1/2}-2x^{-2} \\right) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{4}(\\sqrt{x} - \\frac{2}{x^{2}})dx &= \\int_{1}^{4}x^{1/2}dx - 2\\int_{1}^{4}x^{-2}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{2}{3}x^{3/2}\\right|_{1}^{4} + \\left.2x^{-1}\\right|_{1}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(4^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}) + 2(4^{-1} - 1^{-1}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(8 - 1) + 2(\\frac{1}{4} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{3} + 2 \\cdot (-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{14}{3} - \\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{19}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115054 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115054 1071 "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115054 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using\n\n$$\n9={\\color{red}3}^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n27 = {\\color{red}3}^3\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{2(3x)} = 3^{3(x + 2)}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3x) &= 3(x + 2) \\\\\n6x &= 3x + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115054 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={\\color{red}3}^2$ and $27 = {\\color{red}3}^3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(3x)}={3}^{3(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3x) & =3(x+2) \\\\ 6x & =3x+6 \\\\ 3x & =6 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115055 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} -3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115055 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} -3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115055 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}=-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/07/074a330c55b63458fe94ed36b0c569ecfc6ce8fd839b8566f1dd589440c59c69.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = \\infty.\n$$\n\nHowever, the negative factor $-3$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = -\\infty.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115055 1074 "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\nlim_(x → - ∞) - 3(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} =-3 \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{2})^{x}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](images/07/074a330c55b63458fe94ed36b0c569ecfc6ce8fd839b8566f1dd589440c59c69.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, the negative factor $-3$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = -\\infty\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115056 1071 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x <                2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115056 1071 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115056 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{3}) = 1.230959… \\approx 1.2310\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\n![](images/50/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - 1.2310 \\\\\n&= 5.0522\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115056 1074 "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=1.230\\,959…≈1.231\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 1.231\\,0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$\n\n![](images/50/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.231\\,0 \\\\  & =5.052\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115057 1071 "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x <              2\\pi.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115057 1071 "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115057 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})=0.201\\,357…≈0.201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.201.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 0.201.$ Since $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 0.201.$\n\n![](images/8b/8bcd1ea412f9235a330f37ee9059a023d0ce9e03e2c38388875dc0c12afebc1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.201 \\\\  & ≈2.941\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x = 0.2, 2.9.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115057 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{\\csc x}{5} &= 1 \\\\\n\\csc x &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\ntherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin (\\frac{1}{5}) = 0.201357… \\approx 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.201\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\nSince $\\sin{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 0.201\n$$\n\n![](images/8b/8bcd1ea412f9235a330f37ee9059a023d0ce9e03e2c38388875dc0c12afebc1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π - 0.201 \\\\\n&\\approx 2.941\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are\n\n$$\nx = 0.2, 2.9\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115058 1071 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x <           2\\pi.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115058 1071 "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x < 2\\pi$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115058 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1\n$$\n\nand the value\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nis outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nhas no solution." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115058 1074 "Recall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1,$ and the value $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ has no solution." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115059 1071 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation  $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x <                         2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115059 1071 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115059 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cot x &= 9 - \\cot x \\\\\n3\\cot x &= 9 \\\\\n\\cot x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = arctan(\\frac{1}{3}) = 0.321750 \\approx 0.3218\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0.321\\,8\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + π \\\\\n&= π + 0.3218 \\\\\n&= 3.4634\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115059 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3} .\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{1}{3})=0.321\\,750≈0.321\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.321\\,8.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi +0.321\\,8 \\\\  & =3.463\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115062 1071 "Find $AB-CB$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115062 1071 "Find $AB-CB,$ where\n![](images/90/90f675ec2a211b6157f040735d551d083c770ac33b5dbfcd74b3678b16d5df53.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115062 1074 "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - C &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&=-I_{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB - CB &= (A - C)B \\\\\n&=-I_{3}B \\\\\n&=-B \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115062 1074 "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB-CB=(A-C)B.\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $A-C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-C & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB-CB & =(A-C)B \\\\  & =-{I}_{3}B \\\\  & =-B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3} & 45 \\\\ -81 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115063 1071 "Calculate $ABC$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115063 1071 "Calculate $ABC,$ where\n![](images/de/de98e19bed31050fd07f9979f0428ed4eb90ce05eaff7ba4d19ea81bbd664422.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115063 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC)\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore,\n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nABC &= A(BC) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115063 1074 "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC = (AB)C = A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3 \\times 1$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that will require finding $9$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3 \\times 1$ matrix that will require finding only $3$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $BC$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $A$ by $BC\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =A(BC) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115064 1071 "Given that $A, B$, and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n1. $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n2. $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n3. $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115064 1071 "Given that $A, B,$ and $C$ are $6 \\times 6$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $A(BC) = (AB)C$\n\n- $A(B+C) = BA+CA$\n\n- $A(B+C) = AB+AC$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115064 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n\n$$\nAB + AC  \\neq BA + CA.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115064 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C = A(BC)$ in general.\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B+C) = AB + AC$ in general. However, since matrix multiplication is not commutative, we have\n$AB + AC \\neq BA + CA$.\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C = AC+BC$, and $A(B+C) = AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115065 1071 "Find $A^4$ given that ![](images/45/45b13bcb5bbd8dc54e605acf6d378f582dcb7a4f2ecfff35a7a5343ee30f0515.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115065 1071 "Find $A^4$ given that $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115065 1074 "First, to find $A^2$, we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{2} &= A \\cdot A \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nA^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{4} &= A^{2} \\cdot A^{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115065 1074 "First, to find $A^2,$ we multiply $A$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A^4 = A^2 \\cdot A^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{4} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot {A}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}81 & 0 \\\\ 100 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115066 1071 "Consider the matrix\n![](images/a5/a58c9caa0812b56d74f38a457c3f39e72a3fd365a881d4128cfd55c1773e05f6.png)\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115066 1071 "Consider the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind $f(D)$ if $f(X)=-X^2+3X+2$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115066 1074 "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(X) &= -X^{2} + 3X + 2 \\\\\n&=-X^{2} + 3X + 2I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find\n\n$$\nf(D)=-D^2+3D+2I\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $D^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD^{2} &= D \\cdot D \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(D) &= -D^{2} + 3D + 2I \\\\\n&=-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix} + 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 + 3 + 2 & 10 + 15 + 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 - 9 + 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 25 \\\\ 0 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115066 1074 "First, we rewrite the polynomial, interpreting any constants as multiples of the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(X) & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2 \\\\  & =-{X}^{2}+3X+2I\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find $f(D)=-D^2+3D+2I.$\n\nLet's calculate $D^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can evaluate $f(D)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(D) & =-{D}^{2}+3D+2I \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 15 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+3+2 & 10+15+0 \\\\ 0 & -9-9+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 25 \\\\ 0 & -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115067 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115067 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115067 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)(3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1) &= 3^{2(1)} + 2^{3(1)} + 1 \\\\\n&= 9 + 8 + 1 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115067 1074 "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}({3}^{2x}+{2}^{3x}+1) & ={3}^{2(1)}+{2}^{3(1)}+1 \\\\  & =9+8+1 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115068 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=5 \\\\ -5x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115068 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${2x = 5; - 5x + 6y =-8$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115068 1074 "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. The variable $y$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0$. So, we get\n\n$$\n{2x + 0y = 5; - 5x + 6y =-8\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115068 1074 "First, let's write the system so that both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation. The variable $y$ does not appear in the first equation, but this just means its coefficient is $0.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+0y=5 \\\\ -5x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/cd/cdadd8a9c68f58c4d8434e658d4acffeba524850f361fcd3d6fe3524b3d781a1.png)\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n![](images/35/35104266eb5a086bef0536640ec1310532cc136a251049a1adcfe4418ef0ef89.png)\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n![](images/aa/aa4de061ca95088763e7e5fec289dbd2e5bd298e8986ef6660617ea0ba227821.png)\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ becomes\n\n![](images/10/10781c0f9183c793e3f2de287ede937e764d942767ecc9b64942dc338c1e1307.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115069 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5y=-3x \\\\ 6+x=-2+y\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115069 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n${-5y =-3x; 6 + x =-2 + y$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115069 1074 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-5y=0 \\\\ x-y=-8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n![](images/64/648952bc6e093c08ff29215fce21fe115ab81e9fc3d8b423772123eae31237f3.png)\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n![](images/c1/c1f203be0770c298d9c2c75ccfbfb30354cb314ed75c8b05f6639e5633ca7442.png)\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n![](images/aa/aa4de061ca95088763e7e5fec289dbd2e5bd298e8986ef6660617ea0ba227821.png)\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}$ becomes\n\n![](images/75/75aed8c54a2ec274def967802cbd3b67d0fad3358e676b02cf52e1e8d2cbe0d7.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115069 1074 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order:\n\n$$\n{3x - 5y = 0; x - y =-8\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nbecomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115070 1071 "What is the system of equations represented by\n![](images/6f/6f0ddb43928e0d9f52ca2fe0f0ab578adfa986cba8179746bd34a50a1a7c506b.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115070 1071 "What is the system of equations represented by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -15 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115070 1074 "The  $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y,$ while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10x-15y=5 \\\\ 1x+0y=-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=1 \\\\ x=-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115070 1074 "The $2\\times 2$ matrix represents the coefficients of $x$ and $y$, while the $2\\times 1$ column vector on the right-hand side represents the constants of the equations. So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{10x - 15y = 5; 1x + 0y =-2\n$$\n\nSimplifying both equations in the system, we get\n\n$$\n{2x - 3y = 1; x =-2\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115071 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](images/0b/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115071 1071 "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) =0$\n2. The system $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $A^{-1}$ does not exist" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115071 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/79/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115071 1074 "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115072 1071 "Let ![](images/15/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](images/18/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115072 1071 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n3. The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115072 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/43/43da43be5ab47c640387f77d3b61555d79e9b9a2d61f49d00b56e45a6856bdec.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115072 1074 "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n$det(A)|= det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}; = 1 \\cdot k - (-2) \\cdot (-2); = k - 4$,\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4$. So, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$.\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115073 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115073 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$.*\n\n1. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n2. $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n3. $\\det(A) = 0$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115073 1074 "Recall that given ![](images/db/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115073 1074 "Recall that given $\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:\n\n1. $A$ is invertible.\n2. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$.\n3. The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n4. The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n5. $\\det(A) \\neq 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115075 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115075 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115075 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞) - 5(\\frac{1}{3})^{1 + x} &= -5lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{1 + x} \\\\\n&=-5lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{1} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} \\\\\n&=-5lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{3} \\cdot lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](images/81/81be7f2983aeb647a8fa88e2caad8cea3c2052190222670838077e760a2e7fda.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115075 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1+x} & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1+x} \\\\  & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x.$\n\n![](images/81/81be7f2983aeb647a8fa88e2caad8cea3c2052190222670838077e760a2e7fda.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0,\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty}   \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115076 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/a5/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115076 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n$x + 5y + z = 2; x + 5y + 2z = 5$.\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115076 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/48/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/81/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115076 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=R_{1} + (-1)R_{2}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx + 5y =-1; z = 3\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z$, and we have one free variable, $y$. That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y$.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{x =-1 - 5y; y = y; z = 3\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 - 5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}, y ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115077 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115077 1071 "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n${2x - 2y = 1,; 4x + ky =-3$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115077 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/f4/f4478e5828da7c79a47bf3ca212ed15357befe7b0b208c78beb0895b445272c9.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](images/cf/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115077 1074 "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k + 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n2x - 2y = 1; (k + 4)y =-5\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4$, then the second equation is $0=-5$, which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115078 1071 "Is\n$E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nan elementary matrix?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115078 1071 "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115078 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115078 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115079 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115079 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n![](images/65/65e27c97ac04ff5514c6e1be3b010e7a6efa464131bfbad03a569f85e728ac85.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115079 1074 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186115079 1074 "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n![](images/cd/cd8839007e548fcd0858ebc42904d3659d639da834b8caa476c4765b5b1a6b69.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186115080 1071 "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$, whe