ius of curvature at\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(\\frac{π}{2}) &= (1)/(κ(\\frac{π}{2})) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119583 1074 "The curvature $\\kappa(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t) \\, \\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t) = \\dfrac {|x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)|} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t) = \\dfrac 1 {\\kappa(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 0|}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})|}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t = \\dfrac{\\pi}2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{κ(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119584 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119584 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $y = x^3$ at $x=-1.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119584 1074 "The curvature $\\kappa(x)$ of a plane curve $y = y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(x) = \\dfrac {\\mid y''(x) \\mid} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $y'(x)$ and 