id black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+\\cos^2 t)^3}}, \\quad t\\neq 0$.\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119585 1074 "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) &= 1, & y^{′}(t)|= \\cos t \\\\ x^{″}(t)|= 0, & y^{″}(t)|=-\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(t) &= (1 \\cdot (-\\sin t) - 0 \\cdot \\cos t)/((1^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2})^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-\\sin t)/((1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= (-sin ⁡ t)/(\\sqrt{(1 + \\cos^{2} t)^{3}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119585 1074 "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\left \\langle x(t), y(t) \\right \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\nk (t) = \\dfrac {x'(t) y''(t) - y'(t) x''(t)} {\\big([x'(t)]^2 + [y'(t)]^2\\big)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{({1}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119591 1071 "How can we best describe the following matrix?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119591 1071 "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n![](images/fc/fc2ed708d717844ca73b7ecbfd8467923fad091ae524153350e7e2fb3df1ea4e.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119591 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n![](images/52/5260137f2dbf20b44400659be8467da333ff228e5bff323465f920a9505ee5f2.png)\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal: $a_{21}=6 \\neq 0.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119591 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal:\n\n$$\na_{21}=6 \\neq 0\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119592 1071 "Which of the following matrices are lower triangular matrices?\n\n![](images/ad/ad9636de9b8c93338350a6b99d88476b481ed1bf03a5dd64935abedbdb626524.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119592 1071 "Which of the following matrices are lower triangular matrices?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119592 1074 "The matrix $B$ is a lower triangular matrix since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nThe matrices $A$ and $C$ are *not* lower triangular matrices since they each have a non-zero element above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{23} &= 1 \\ne 0 \\\\\nc_{13} &= 1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119592 1074 "The matrix $B$ is a lower triangular matrix since all the entries above the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nThe matrices $A$ and $C$ are *not* lower triangular matrices since they each have a non-zero element above the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23} & =1\\ne 0 \\\\ {c}_{13} & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119593 1071 "Calculate\n![](images/15/15738eec7022be44c90577b931f2e8aa8446a8fc3f911260fcf468ae6ddd2d57.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119593 1071 "Calculate\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119593 1074 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\cdot C_{41} + 0 \\cdot C_{42} + 0 \\cdot C_{43} + (-1) \\cdot C_{44} \\\\\n&= (-1) \\cdot (-1)^{4 + 4} \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} &= 1 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+ 0 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (3 - 8) + (0 - 0) \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119593 1074 "The $4$th row of the matrix contains three zeros, more than any other row. So using a Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{41}+0\\cdot {C}_{42}+0\\cdot {C}_{43}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{44} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3 \\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-8)+(0-0) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/ce/ce96c053abb756977f717e7130257af39307e5f663596042ddcb5b14b0153cb8.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119594 1071 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n![](images/82/82b151cbc7d19f4afa375763d0ca8eb281d76e11f2d775c068e97dbf3d40cae4.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119594 1071 "Using a cofactor expansion along the $2$nd column, write the following determinant as a combination of $3 \\times 3$ determinants:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119594 1074 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\cdot C_{12} + 3 \\cdot C_{22} + 0 \\cdot C_{32} + 0 \\cdot C_{42} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (-1)^{2 + 2} \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119594 1074 "Using a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{32}+0\\cdot {C}_{42} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/ef/efb849ac6e240be4d0a6aac3e7f9405158b8b1bb996956b7346c02b5bee751b1.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119595 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119595 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\det (A) = -16.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ (switching the first and the second rows). What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119595 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= -det(A) \\\\\n&= - (-16) \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119595 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-16) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119596 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119596 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $6 \\times 6$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 32.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_3:= \\dfrac{1}{8} R_3$. What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119596 1074 "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= \\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\cdot 32 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119596 1074 "The row operation applied to $A$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\dfrac{1}{8}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =\\frac{1}{8}det(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119597 1071 "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$, $R_6:= 4 R_6$, and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$. What is the value of $\\det(B)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119597 1071 "Suppose $A$ is an $8 \\times 8$ matrix and $\\det(A) = 5.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5,$ $R_6:= 4 R_6,$ and $R_8: = R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7.$ What is the value of $\\det(B)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119597 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =4\\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\  & =-4det(A) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119597 1074 "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation $R_4 \\leftrightarrow R_5$ involves swapping two rows, so it changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\n- The operation $R_6:= 4 R_6$ involves multiplying a row by $4$, so the determinant is multiplied by $4$ as well.\n- The operation $R_8:= R_8 - \\dfrac{1}{2} R_7$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row, so it does not change the determinant.\n\nTherefore, we have to change the sign of the determinant to the opposite one and multiply by $4$ as well. Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(B) &= 4 \\cdot (-det(A)) \\\\\n&=-4det(A) \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119598 1071 "Given that ![](images/bc/bcebda30c5b3a878271e607bc77f04612470812126f5916469147d884516dc20.png) find ![](images/e0/e0f9783da49e275b641d727e32506542449fe6be9db636fd1b6cc6007704b500.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119598 1071 "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ find $\\det(A)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 fals