nce $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119610 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119611 1071 "Given that\n![](images/d4/d4fa85cab74f4bda57059d1c6abc9dacea665531ce84ba9d2f1598a13c49ef60.png) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186119611 1071 "Given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119611 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U$, we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{2} & ⟺\\end{vmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(E_{1}) \\cdot A \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= U\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U$. Now, since $E_1A=U$, we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{1}A &= U \\\\\nA &= E_{1}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}U \\\\\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(L)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(U)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186119611 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124267 1071 "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124267 1071 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124267 1074 "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y = x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2x))/(x^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{2}}; = 3$.\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y =-x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2(-x)))/((-x)^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{-x^{2}}{x^{2}}; =-1$.\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124267 1074 "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124268 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124268 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $g(x, y) =3x^2+4xy$ at the point $A(0,1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,4).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124268 1074 "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v$.\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= ⟨1, 4⟩ - ⟨0, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 3⟩ \\\\\nu &= (AB^(→))/(‖AB^(→)‖) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1^{2} + 3^{2}}}⟨1, 3⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1, 3⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇g(x, y) &= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} + 4xy), \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} + 4xy)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6x + 4y, 4x⟩ \\\\\n∇g(0, 1) &= ⟨6 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1, 4 \\cdot 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨4, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_{u}^{′}(0, 1) &= ∇g(0, 1) \\cdot u \\\\\n&= ⟨4, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124268 1074 "The directional derivative $g'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b)$ of a function $g(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $\\mathbf v$ is given by\n\n$$\ng'_{\\mathbf u}(a,b) = \\nabla g(a,b)\\cdot\\mathbf u\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,4⟩-⟨0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}⟨1,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+4xy),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+4xy)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6x+4y,\\,4x⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1) & =⟨6\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1,\\,4\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(0,1) & =∇g(0,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨4,0⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124269 1071 "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124269 1071 "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124269 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- ∇f(x, y) &= -\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{2}y))i - \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{2}y))j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot 2xyi - \\frac{1}{x^{2}y} \\cdot x^{2}j \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x}i - \\frac{1}{y}j \\\\\n- ∇f(2, 1) &= -\\frac{2}{2}i - \\frac{1}{1}j \\\\\n&=-i - j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-\\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124269 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}y))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{ }i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124270 1071 "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right)$. What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124270 1071 "Consider the function $g (x, y) = \\ln \\left(\\sqrt {xy^3}\\right).$ What is the rate of change of $g$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction in which the function increases most rapidly?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124270 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇g(x, y) &= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (\\sqrt{xy^{3}})), \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (\\sqrt{xy^{3}}))⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy^{3}}} \\cdot \\frac{y^{3}}{2\\sqrt{xy^{3}}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{xy^{3}}} \\cdot \\frac{3xy^{2}}{2\\sqrt{xy^{3}}}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2x}, \\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\\n∇g(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}) &= ⟨(1)/(2 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})), (3)/(2 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}))⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nin the direction of\n\n$$\n\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2})‖ &= ‖⟨1, 1⟩‖ \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1^{2} + 1^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124270 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124271 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124271 1071 "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124271 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y) = (1,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2y^{2} + x)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 0 + 1)i + (0 + 4y + 0)j \\\\\n&= (2x + 1)i + 4yj \\\\\n∇f(1, 1) &= (2(1) + 1)i + 4(1)j \\\\\n&= 3i + 4j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∇f(1, 1))/(‖∇f(1, 1)‖) &= \\frac{3i + 4j}{\\sqrt{3^{2} + 4^{2}}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(3i + 4j)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124271 1074 "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124272 1071 "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$. Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124272 1071 "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124272 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} - 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1\n$$\n\nour curve can be viewed as the level curve\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= f_{x}(x, y)i + f_{y}(x, y)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} - 1)i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x^{2}}{9} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} - 1)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x}{9}i + \\frac{y}{8}j \\\\\n∇f(0, 4) &= (2(0))/(9)i + ((4))/(8)j \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}j \\\\\n&= ⟨0, \\frac{1}{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4)$.\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{y - y_{0}}{x - x_{0}} = \\frac{v_{y}}{v_{x}}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{y}(x - x_{0}) &= v_{x}(y - y_{0})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}(x - 0) &= 0(y - 4) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1\n$$\n\nat the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124272 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124273 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124273 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124273 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing\n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$.\n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x^{2} - xy - z^{2})k \\\\\n&= (4x - y)i - xj - 2zk \\\\\n∇f(2, 3, \\sqrt{2}) &= (4 \\cdot 2 - 3)i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k \\\\\n&= 5i - 2j - 2\\sqrt{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124273 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124274 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124274 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124274 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) \\\\\n0 &= \\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z\n$$\n\nour surface can be viewed as the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point\n\n$$\n\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)\n$$\n\ncan be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}(x, y, z)i + f_{y}(x, y, z)j + f_{z}(x, y, z)k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{2} - xy) - z)k \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2x - y}{x^{2} - xy})i + (\\frac{1}{y - x})j - k \\\\\n∇f(2, 1, \\ln 2) &= (\\frac{2 \\cdot 2 - 1}{2^{2} - 2 \\cdot 1})i + (\\frac{1}{1 - 2})j - k \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}i - j - k \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nand passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{x - x_{0}}{v_{x}} = \\frac{y - y_{0}}{v_{y}} = \\frac{z - z_{0}}{v_{z}}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n(x - 2)/((\\frac{3}{2})) = \\frac{y - 1}{-1} = \\frac{z - \\ln 2}{-1}; \\frac{x - 2}{3} = \\frac{y - 1}{-2} = \\frac{z - \\ln 2}{-2}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124274 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124275 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx$, find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124275 1071 "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac yx,$ find the set of values $(x,y)$ on which $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124275 1074 "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined.\n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0$. So, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{y}(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0$. So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\,: \\, x\\ne 0 \\}\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{f}∩D_{f_{y}} &= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}∩{(x, y): x \\ne 0} \\\\\n&= {(x, y): x \\ne 0}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nexists on the set\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,: \\, x \\neq 0 \\}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124275 1074 "Remember that the partial derivative of a function exists where the original function is defined *and* the partial derivative is defined. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x,y)$ is not defined when $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f$ is \n\n$$\nD_f = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivative of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ is also not defined for $x=0.$ So, the domain of $f_y$ is\n\n$$\nD_{f_y} = \\{(x,y) \\, : \\, x\\ne 0 \\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the set where  $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists, we take the intersection of the domain of the original function $(D_f)$ with the domain of the partial derivative $(D_{f_y})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{f}\\cap {D}_{{f}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ne 0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ exists on the set $\\{ (x,y)\\, : \\, x \\neq 0 \\}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124276 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}$?\n\n1. $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n2. $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n3. $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124276 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x,y,z)=xz\\ln{(x+y+z)}?$\n\n- $f_x(-1,-1,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_y(1,0,-1)$ is defined\n\n- $f_z(1,1,1)$ is defined" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124276 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= z\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= \\frac{xz}{x + y + z} \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z} &= x\\ln (x + y + z) + \\frac{xz}{x + y + z}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f$, we get\n$f(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)}$,\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y$, we get\n$f(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}$,\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)$\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)$:\n$f_{z}(1, 1, 1)|= (1)\\ln (1 + 1 + 1) + ((1)(1))/(1 + 1 + 1) = \\ln (3) + \\frac{1}{3}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124276 1074 "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y,z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{xz}{x+y+z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+\\frac{xz}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false. If we substitute $(-1,-1,-1)$ into $f,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(-1,-1,-1) = (-1)(-1)\\ln{(-1-1-1)} = \\ln{(-3)},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since the logarithm exists only for positive arguments. Therefore, $f_x$ cannot exist at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0,-1)$ into $f_y,$ we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1) = \\dfrac{(1)(-1)}{1+0-1} = \\dfrac{-1}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since we have division by zero. Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(1,1,1)= (1)(1)\\ln(1+1+1)=\\ln(3)\n$$\n\nLikewise $f_z$ is also defined at $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(1,1,1) & =(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+1+1)+\\frac{(1)(1)}{1+1+1}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124282 1071 "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124282 1071 "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124282 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})j \\\\\n&= 2xi + 6y^{2}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(r(t)) &= 2(\\sin t)i + 6(e^{t})^{2}j \\\\\n&= 2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t})j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + e^{t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= ∇f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= (2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j) \\cdot (\\cos ti + e^{t}j) \\\\\n&= 2\\sin t\\cos t + 6e^{2t} \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\sin 2t + 6e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124282 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124283 1071 "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124283 1071 "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t \\, \\mathbf j +t^3 \\, \\mathbf k$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124283 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\ln y)i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\ln y)j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\ln y)k \\\\\n&= z\\ln yi + \\frac{xz}{y}j + x\\ln yk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2$, $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(r(t)) &= t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= 2ti + j + 3t^{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= ∇f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk) \\cdot (2ti + j + 3t^{2}k) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln t) \\cdot 2t + (t^{4}) \\cdot 1 + (t^{2}\\ln t) \\cdot 3t^{2} \\\\\n&= 2t^{4}\\ln t + t^{4} + 3t^{4}\\ln t \\\\\n&= t^{4}(5\\ln t + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124283 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124284 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124284 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124284 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\nTo calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y) &= ⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} - y^{2} - z), \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} - y^{2} - z), \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} - y^{2} - z)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute\n\n$$\nx = \\sin t, y=\\cos t\n$$\n\nand $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(r(t)) &= ⟨2\\sin t,-2\\cos t,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t), \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t, 1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= ∇f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) \\\\\n&= ⟨2\\sin t,-2\\cos t,-1⟩ \\cdot ⟨\\cos t,-\\sin t, 1⟩ \\\\\n&= 2\\sin t \\cdot \\cos t - 2\\cos t \\cdot (-\\sin t) - 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 2\\sin t\\cos t + 2\\sin t\\cos t - 1 \\\\\n&= 4\\sin t\\cos t - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(\\frac{π}{4}))] &= 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{4})\\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) - 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124284 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124289 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/50/5036479a948abee5644cd3b96632f6aaf8ee370f7473ba27e11d25bb84ddb781.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124289 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n![](images/50/5036479a948abee5644cd3b96632f6aaf8ee370f7473ba27e11d25bb84ddb781.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that: \n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV  shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124290 1071 "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $(-3,-1]$\n\n- $(0,4]$\n\n- $[-2,2)$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124290 1071 "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n1. $(-3,-1]$\n2. $(0,4]$\n3. $[-2,2)$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124290 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,-1]$. Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/24/245484a6a77160a5f5892738a7e1f8e488f6df8e4ba990bbc94ae964c3305525.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $(0,4]$ and $[-2,2)$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/47/4737a1460e1abd4edc200404407fef10761f038d9a29e52ba0f8c3e8c69bb93f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124290 1074 "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.   \n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-3,-1].$  Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/24/245484a6a77160a5f5892738a7e1f8e488f6df8e4ba990bbc94ae964c3305525.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $(0,4]$ and $[-2,2)$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/47/4737a1460e1abd4edc200404407fef10761f038d9a29e52ba0f8c3e8c69bb93f.png)\n\n          \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124291 1071 "Given that $f(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n2. $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n3. $x \\in (-1,4]$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124291 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124291 1074 "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2\n$$\n\ncan be obtained in the following way:\n\n1. Take the curve $y= \\mid x \\mid$.\n2. Translate it by $2$ units to the right, to get $y= \\mid x-2 \\mid$.\n3. Finally, translate $y= \\mid x-2 \\mid$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2$.\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2]$. Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4]$. On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124291 1074 "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124293 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $c=1$ gives a circle\n2. $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n3. $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124293 1071 "The level curves of the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+2y^2-2}{x^2}$ are given by $f(x,y) = c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $c=1$ gives a circle\n\n- $c=0$ gives an ellipse\n\n- $c=2$ gives a hyperbola" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124293 1074 "Setting\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = c\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2}{x^{2}} &= c \\\\\nx^{2} + 2y^{2} - 2 &= cx^{2} \\\\\n(1 - c)x^{2} + 2y^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1$, then the level curve is $y^2 = 1$, which consists of two lines.\n- If $c = 0$, then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2$, which is an ellipse.\n- If $c = 2$, then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124293 1074 "Setting $f(x,y) = c$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2}{{x}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2 & =c{x}^{2} \\\\ (1-c){x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c = 1,$ then the level curve is $y^2 = 1,$ which consists of two lines.\n\n- If $c = 0,$ then the level curve is $x^2+2y^2=2,$ which is an ellipse.\n\n- If $c = 2,$ then the level curve is $2y^2-x^2=2$ which is a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statements II and III are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124294 1071 "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124294 1071 "Sketch the level curves of the surface $z=e^{x^2-y^2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124294 1074 "The level curves for the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}\n$$\n\ntake the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\ne^{x^{2} - y^{2}} = c; \\ln (e^{x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\ln c; x^{2} - y^{2} = \\ln c\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c$.\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c$.\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0$. In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x$.\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0$. In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c$.\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2ec43639cb31e03cc4a00e43969c7f0333c098fc4b366ea1609772faabfda5.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124294 1074 "The level curves for the function $f(x,y)=e^{x^2-y^2}$ take the form\n\n$$\ne^{x^2-y^2} = c.\n$$\n\nNote that we must have the constraint $c\\gt 0$ since $e^x \\gt 0$ for all $x.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can split this into three cases, depending on the sign of $\\ln c.$\n\n- Case 1: $\\ln c \\gt 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $x^2-y^2 = \\ln c.$\n\n- Case 2: $\\ln c = 0.$ In this case, we have the lines $y=\\pm x.$\n\n- Case 3: $\\ln c < 0.$ In this case, we have the family of hyperbolas $y^2 - x^2 = -\\ln c.$\n\nTherefore, the level curves are as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5f2ec43639cb31e03cc4a00e43969c7f0333c098fc4b366ea1609772faabfda5.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124296 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its pivot columns?\n![](images/bc/bc54c8852a673014f5bb9c7f3b7c07b5f2414d8edf0cea79e2b410211e97ae20.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124296 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $B$ is given below. What are its pivot columns?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124296 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n![](images/c3/c31f9eb258350b5f9cc63d2e96a3e68122428b64e097451d7b9a12a3b58750c3.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124296 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $B$ has $3$ pivots in the $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124297 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $C$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n![](images/bb/bbc4e9e0d0497930f434cd3dade35973105401ba72c74690d924c6390d87413d.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124297 1071 "The row echelon form matrix $C$ is given below. What are its **non**-pivot columns?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124297 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n![](images/da/da73e68fe8bc5e6baa8876beb17d702707cb0ad43831f42ecfa45f3a98d43202.png)\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124297 1074 "Remember that the pivots of a matrix in row echelon form are the first non-zero entries in each row of the matrix.\n\nWe can see that $C$ has $3$ pivots in the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only non-pivot column is the $2$nd column." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124298 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124298 1071 "Determine the number of pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/eb/eb0fc447f75650bb47da114b8cd4f0986b5f501333f61005e2c1f4989c3a11ed.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124298 1074 "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124298 1074 "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots, and therefore the number of pivot columns is $3.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124299 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124299 1071 "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $B,$ given by\n![](images/b5/b57dc02f6baddfcb517ccf12366320534074b33814577b4a2d1010377325fc19.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186124299 1074 "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 2R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & R_{4}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{4} + (-\\frac{2}{7})R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186124299 1074 "First, we need to convert $B$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{2}{7}){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $B$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $3$rd, and $4$th columns." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128899 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128899 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y^2+z^2,$ find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,1).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128899 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = k\n$$\n\nat the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at\n\n$$\n(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x, y, z) &= f_{x}i + f_{y}j + f_{z}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})i + \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})j + \\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2})k \\\\\n&= 2xi + 2yj + 2zk \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) &= 2(2)i + 2(-2)j + 2(1)k \\\\\n&= 4i - 4j + 2k \\\\\n&= ⟨4,-4, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x_{0}, y_{0}, z_{0}) \\cdot ⟨x - x_{0}, y - y_{0}, z - z_{0}⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n∇f(2,-2, 1) \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y - (-2), z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n⟨4,-4, 2⟩ \\cdot ⟨x - 2, y + 2, z - 1⟩ &= 0 \\\\\n4(x - 2) - 4(y + 2) + 2(z - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n4x - 4y + 2z - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n2x - 2y + z - 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128899 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0,z_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0, \\: z-z_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(x_0,y_0,z_0) =(2,-2,1)\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}\\,i+{f}_{y}\\,j+{f}_{z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1) & =2(2)\\text{ }i+2(-2)\\text{ }j+2(1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j+2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-4\\,,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇\\,f(2,-2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y-(-2),\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨4,\\,-4,\\,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4(x-2)-4(y+2)+2(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 4x-4y+2z-18 & =0 \\\\ 2x-2y+z-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128900 1071 "The point $P$ is located in the second quadrant and lies on the unit circle.\nThe $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $P$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128900 1071 "The point $P$ is located in the second quadrant and lies on the unit circle. \nThe $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128900 1074 "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\nx &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128900 1074 "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$ into the above and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. Therefore, $x = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128901 1071 "![](images/22/2262bdc869d10cef3cc7a779365f5afa0eb712676a397e39d248c813b305cbf0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{3}{4}$, what is the value of $\\cos \\theta$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128901 1071 "![](images/22/2262bdc869d10cef3cc7a779365f5afa0eb712676a397e39d248c813b305cbf0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{3}{4},$ what is the value of $\\cos \\theta?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128901 1074 "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ &= 1 - \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{9}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt {7}}{4}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128901 1074 "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{3}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt {7}}{4}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128902 1071 "![](images/0c/0c29b3961f3e1178f0c82680ca3f67904e57b6fe2cc8b887f09926fcf6c2149a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is the value of $\\tan\\theta$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128902 1071 "![](images/0c/0c29b3961f3e1178f0c82680ca3f67904e57b6fe2cc8b887f09926fcf6c2149a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is the value of $\\tan\\theta?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128902 1074 "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ &= 1 - \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-\\sqrt 3\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128902 1074 "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So, $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-\\sqrt 3.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128903 1071 "![](images/0e/0e88dc5962fc5bd128c0de5502e8dcd1edf34353de1d31c2d8a21ac672633644.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is $\\csc\\theta$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128903 1071 "![](images/0e/0e88dc5962fc5bd128c0de5502e8dcd1edf34353de1d31c2d8a21ac672633644.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2},$ what is $\\csc\\theta?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128903 1074 "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-2\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128903 1074 "We're given that $x=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-2.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128904 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3$.\n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0$, but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128904 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -3}f(x) \\neq f(3)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity. A similar argument applies for $x=3$, which is also a removable discontinuity. So, the function has removable discontinuities at $x=-3, 3.$ \n\nNote that the function also has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a removable discontinuity. Checking the conditions, we find that\n\n- $f(0)= -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=0$ is not a removable discontinuity." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128905 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128905 1074 "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x) \\neq f(2)$ $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a point discontinuity." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128906 1071 "Given the function $f(x)$, defined by\n${x + 3,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; 3 - x, \\mid x > 0$,\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n3. $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128906 1071 "Given the function $f(x),$ defined by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3, & x<0 \\\\ 0, & x=0 \\\\ 3-x, & x>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^+} f(x)=\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0^-} f(x)$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)=f(0)$\n\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128906 1074 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+3)=3, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3-x)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3,$ while the value of the function is $f(0)=0.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128906 1074 "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{-})(x + 3) = 3,; lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) = lim_(x → 0^{+})(3 - x) = 3$.\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n$\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3. \\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- The value of the limit is $\\displaystyle\\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x) = 3$, while the value of the function is $f(0)=0$. So, statement II is false and\n$\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x\\to 0} f(x)\\neq f(0)\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=0$, and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128907 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$. Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3$, but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128907 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128908 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1$, but this is not a jump discontinuity." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128908 1074 "The function has a jump discontinuity 