infty, 2)$, $(2,3)$, and $(3, \\infty)$, namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2)\\cup(2,3)\\cup(3, \\infty)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on these intervals." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128914 1074 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\neq 3$.\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty, 2),$ $(2,3),$ and $(3, \\infty),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2)\\cup(2,3)\\cup(3, \\infty)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on these intervals." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128915 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2+1$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128915 1071 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2+1.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128915 1074 "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm, and a polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial $x^2+1$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function $\\log(2x-3)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2 + 1$ is continuous on the interval $\\left(\\dfrac 32,\\infty\\right).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128915 1074 "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm, and a polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial $x^2+1$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function $\\log(2x-3)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x - 3 & > 0 \\\\ 2x & > 3 \\\\ x & > \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2 + 1\n$$\n\nis continuous on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 32,\\infty\\right)\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128916 1071 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi$. What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x) = \\sin{x}+\\cot{x}$ is continuous?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128916 1071 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x) = \\sin{x}+\\cot{x}$ is continuous?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128916 1074 "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\sin{x}$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,2\\pi]$, but the function $\\cot{x}$ is not defined at\n\n$$\nx=0, \\pi, 2\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(0, \\pi)$ and $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$, namely\n\n$$\n(0, \\pi)\\cup(\\pi, 2\\pi)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128916 1074 "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\sin{x}$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,2\\pi],$ but the function $\\cot{x}$ is not defined at $x=0, \\pi, 2\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(0, \\pi)$ and $(\\pi, 2\\pi),$ namely\n\n$$\n(0, \\pi)\\cup(\\pi, 2\\pi)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128917 1071 "Does the function ![](images/49/49e4cd87824a20b73175305aac7f3773f04a28e02cfa9c3c89cef9079a21ae8a.png) below have a jump discontinuity at ![](images/22/22a0edba1f275de80719ea33fc4878640d2b2b93f8e5620c8999d6a449cdd35b.png)\n\n![](images/74/742066aa035c1b710ab30a74ff5320d3941aae17bbbb8a4c5edcf35ff67c5920.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128917 1071 "Does the function $f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity at $x=0$?\n\n${x^{2} + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ 2x - 1,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128917 1074 "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- ![](images/29/29d8d36954a25bff5808ce7c59dfc3ec0e373d22541cc2df85c93aa0a74c8bb4.png) exists ![](images/a4/a4785336954b0736113ea8ad968aa60db31e55720ff5bdeb128597a8c7c72b90.png)\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n![](images/94/949d5bef0569fbd6f58382e07873412bb07cf3b735ae264c9cb78b366fa59bd2.png)\nwhile\n![](images/d6/d6dfbdbb30c840068ad7a86370f6e444453f925ba8869140b7288b51d164f228.png)\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. ![](images/81/81510388d37345ec3bfb525da6795c62d4ef190798e1574139469b5a90d17d69.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/1d/1d1ac18ca3b92f6df4647c49855e18d40ab7637269ff9b366edbfcaa96c4d59d.png) has a jump discontinuity at ![](images/8f/8f57be1d6e33e5ee1ad485bc58b29dbca4819ee229a505c0e7601150392d7615.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128917 1074 "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^-} 2x-1 = 2(0)-1 = -1$\nwhile\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^+} x^2+1 = 0^2+1 = 1$.\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128921 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\cot\\left(2x-\\dfrac\\pi 6\\right)$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128921 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\cot\\left(2x-\\dfrac\\pi 6\\right).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128921 1074 "Given a function of the form\n\n$$\ny = A\\cot(Bx +C) + D\n$$\n\nthe period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2$. Therefore, the period is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128921 1074 "Given a function of the form $y = A\\cot(Bx +C) + D,$ the period is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=2.$ Therefore, the period is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128922 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x) = \\cot \\left(2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6\\right)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128922 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x) = \\cot \\left(2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6\\right)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128922 1074 "The zeros of $y=\\cot x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\cot \\left(2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6\\right)\n$$\n\nwe replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with\n\n$$\n\\left(2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6\\right)\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + \\frac{π}{6} &= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ \\\\\n2x &= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ - \\frac{π}{6} \\\\\n2x &= \\frac{π}{3} + nπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{nπ}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128922 1074 "The zeros of $y=\\cot x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x) = \\cot \\left(2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x + \\dfrac \\pi 6 =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{n\\pi}{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128923 1071 "What expression gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=\\tan\\left(2x\\right)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128923 1071 "What expression gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\tan\\left(2x\\right)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128923 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\tan x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n \\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(2x\\right)\n$$\n\nwe replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $2x$. This gives\n\n$$\n2x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{π}{4} + \\frac{nπ}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{n\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128923 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\tan x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n \\pi,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\tan\\left(2x\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $2x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{n\\pi}{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128924 1071 "What is the domain of the function $y=\\tan\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128924 1071 "What is the domain of the function $y=\\tan\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128924 1074 "The domain of $y=\\tan x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx \\neq \\dfrac\\pi 2 + n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right)\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 2 \\neq \\dfrac\\pi 2 + n\\pi\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{2} & \\ne \\frac{π}{2} + nπ \\\\ x & \\ne π + 2nπ\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is\n\n$$\nx \\neq \\pi + 2n\\pi\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128924 1074 "The domain of $y=\\tan x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx \\neq \\dfrac\\pi 2 + n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $\\left(\\dfrac x 2\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 2 \\neq  \\dfrac\\pi 2 + n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\pi +2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x \\neq \\pi + 2n\\pi,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128931 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/34/34184a95be2b3ae6865fde109ace7e8b4397f4b01982928be73cff4199792d61.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128931 1071 "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128931 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/b9/b9f56d3f0414f4b56b50e59c2c02d9072f1fb74ce09d801a1ab62abac61ad737.png)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/85/8562a693df46625ee0dc2bcdecc664dfc50a99889fdb855ae2e06dc1db02a3d4.png)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/d2/d263bfe2386309c55f1d63d97d15eaa682e9b0748a9bee6d3378d0b9035927ba.png)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/6c/6ccb00b5e1387cd15847c50d66b26ee903d95428bcff2bfc933b675f9b590717.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128931 1074 "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n2. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128932 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](images/e3/e300a9ac18f0170ae9053c9635fe13a9dd6def793ebfc0bd9dbe81e28810a347.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128932 1071 "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128932 1074 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](images/f3/f3d533c5687da1a75a07c6ad86b40ee8aa6ea5c86cdd250d5c47ff99b885ef61.png)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5d/5d576fca72f6a4f9f0668ff0efbdc2cacc33f8dbb2c00192a7397da9ec83adea.png)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/4f/4f6a034def35760ae94b6eb6a425c59950556a78c124aa9ad0fcfdaa06053b28.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128932 1074 "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n1. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n2. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128933 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](images/08/085cb01a2e4da286ea19a6f1e1707e685646af3d887abb1a57ab50e4d352c53f.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128933 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186128933 1074 "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](images/d7/d7b93aa8abed895e49b965b8d0905088249475cad98757e7a93a174fae143b49.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186128933 1074 "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 11 & 10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133558 1071 "Consider ![](images/ba/ba3f1852b12b6f4a246a855c88c9b867b7e750c6dddf350cab0324629fc047c4.png) and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A,$ is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186133558 1071 "Consider $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$.\nFind the row operation that, when applied to $A$, is equivalent to multiplying $A$ by the given elementary matrix $E$ from the left." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133558 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the second row of $I$ and adding $7$ times the third row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + 7R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary\nrow operation\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+7R_3\n$$\n\nto $A$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133558 1074 "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the second row of $I$ and adding $7$ times the third row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary \nrow operation $R_2 :=R_2+7R_3$ to $A.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186133559 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation $R_2:=R_2-2R_1$ to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$. What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133559 1071 "Suppose we are applying the elementary row operation $R_2:=R_2-2R_1$ to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A.$ What is the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186133559 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2-2R_1\n$$\n\nto $A$.\n\nTo find $E$, we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - 2R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133559 1074 "We need to find a matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation $R_2 := R_2-2R_1$ to $A.$\n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/8d/8d015aca8950073c165d53b14f86f0e65a207438ad901ce82f85f65bb8347519.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186133560 1071 "Suppose we are applying these two elementary row operations, in the given order, to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$:\n$R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3, \\qquad R_1:=6R_1$\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133560 1071 "Suppose we are applying these two elementary row operations, in the given order, to a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nR_2\\leftrightarrow R_3, \\qquad\n\tR_1:=6R_1\n$$\n\nFind the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186133560 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3$. The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n$I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid ↔R_{3}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; = E_{1}$\n- The second operation is $R_1:=6R_1$. The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n$I|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1} \\mid:=6R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = E_{2}$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A$, we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A$, we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186133560 1074 "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is $R_1:=6R_1.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by $E_1$ on the left and get $E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/37/375f1950c460b1f310fa279f6d7f203f88ff641000715dfaec192f47d785aa93.png)\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to $E_1 A,$ we multiply by $E_2$ on the left and get $E_2 E_1 A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/63/6357cc2a2526f38a0f0f7e2e7ef4ee7a1a564adfa70264028ff732f44ba7b763.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186147339 1071 "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\n- Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\n- Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186147339 1071 "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$r(t) = 300t^2+50$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour.\n\n1. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n2. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186147339 1074 "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\nTo find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2$, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}r(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{2}300t^{2} + 50dt \\\\\n&= 100t^{3} + \\left.50t\\right|_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (100 \\cdot 2^{3} + 50(2)) - (0) \\\\\n&= (800 + 100) \\\\\n&= 900\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50\n$$\n\npeople per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t)$, is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{2}R(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{2}r(t) - 25dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}r(t)dt_(⏟)_(900) - \\int_{0}^{2}25dt \\\\\n&= 900 - [25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 900 - 50 \\\\\n&= 850\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186147339 1074 "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186147340 1071 "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186147340 1071 "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 6t^2+2$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186147340 1074 "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(t) &= r(t) - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} + 2 - 6 \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(t) &= T(0) + \\int_{0}^{t}R(x)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + \\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(1) &= 50 + \\int_{0}^{1}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 50 + \\left.[2x^{3} - 4x]\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 50 + [(2(1)^{3} - 4(1)) - (0)] \\\\\n&= 50 - 2 \\\\\n&= 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186147340 1074 "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186147341 1071 "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186147341 1071 "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$T(t) = 50 + \\displaystyle \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,$.\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0$, find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186147341 1074 "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dT}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(50 + \\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(50) + \\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{0}^{t}(6x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}\n$$\n\nand $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0$,\n\n$$\nt=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nmust be the minimum value." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186147341 1074 "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value." 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186175774 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-1}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\,\\,1, & x