 = c\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\log_{2} (3) \\\\\nx\\log_{2} (3) - x &= 1 \\\\\nx(\\log_{2} (3) - 1) &= 1 \\\\\nx &= (1)/(\\log_{2} (3) - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186179511 1074 "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x{log}_{2}⁡(3)-x & =1 \\\\ x({log}_{2}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186179512 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5$?" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186179512 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5?$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186179512 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm\n\n$$\n\\left(\\ln\\right)\n$$\n\nof both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x + 1} &= 2^{x} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n\\ln (e^{x + 1}) &= \\ln (2^{x} \\cdot 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(e^a \\right) = a\n$$\n\nto simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= \\ln (2^{x}) + \\ln (5) \\\\\nx + 1 &= x\\ln (2) + \\ln (5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= x\\ln (2) + \\ln (5) \\\\\nx - x\\ln (2) &= \\ln (5) - 1 \\\\\nx(1 - \\ln (2)) &= \\ln (5) - 1 \\\\\nx &= (\\ln (5) - 1)/(1 - \\ln (2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186179512 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & ={2}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186184058 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}$.\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n$\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20}$,\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186184058 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\