is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186184067 1071 "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186184067 1071 "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186184067 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 2x^{3} - x^{4} \\\\\nf^{′}(x) &= 6x^{2} - 4x^{3} \\\\\nf^{″}(x) &= 12x - 12x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12x - 12x^{2} & > 0 \\\\ 12x^{2} - 12x & < 0 \\\\ x(x - 1) & < 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1)$.\n\n![](images/54/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1$. So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,1)$.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) < 0$. By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1) > 0$, which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(1,\\infty\\right)\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186184067 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](images/54/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <    