{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $B^2=O$, the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186184069 1074 "Computing $B^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since $B^2=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186184070 1071 "Find $C^5$ given that\n![](images/c7/c757e46b9d91c0f57e72fd05d2b5d83558332b6e20fcefdf75d9f0388796da05.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186184070 1071 "Find $C^5$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186184070 1074 "First, we compute $C^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC^{2} &= C \\cdot C \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $C^2=C$, so $C$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute $C^5$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC^{5} &= C^{2} \\cdot C^{2} \\cdot C \\\\\n&= C \\cdot C \\cdot C \\\\\n&= C^{2} \\cdot C \\\\\n&= C \\cdot C \\\\\n&= C^{2} \\\\\n&= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186184070 1074 "First, we compute $C^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $C^2=C,$ so $C$ is an idempotent matrix. \n\nWe can use this fact to compute $C^5$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{5} & ={C}^{2}\\cdot {C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2}\\cdot C \\\\  & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & ={C}^{2} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n![](images/21/213331b37f44266f8bec1e00252499c88b2dc89114f93243a18dd2d1caaaddc9.png)" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186188207 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} =  \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue} 1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = 1.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186188207 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 1 + \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ \\\\ (2) & \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ - 1 \\\\ (3) & \\sec^{2} θ - \\tan^{2} θ = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue} 1} = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec^2 \\theta - \\tan^2 \\theta} = 1\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186188208 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta}$." 13194139533387 17592186045585 false] [17592186188208 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta}.$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186188208 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\sec{\\theta}+\\tan{\\theta}} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}\\sec^2{\\theta} - \\tan^2{\\theta}}{\\sec{\\theta} + \\tan{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta} =  \\sec{\\theta} - \\tan{\\theta}.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 true] [17592186188208 1074 "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1) & 1 + \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ \\\\ (2) & \\tan^{2} θ = \\sec^{2} θ - 1 \\\\ (3) & \\sec^{2} θ - \\tan^{2} θ = 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}1}{\\sec{\\theta}+\\tan{\\theta}} = \\dfrac{\\color{blue}\\sec^2{\\theta} - \\tan^2{\\theta}}{\\sec{\\theta} + \\tan{\\theta}}\n$$\n\nNext, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sec^{2} θ - \\tan^{2} θ}{\\sec θ + \\tan θ} &= ((\\sec θ - \\tan θ)(\\sec θ + \\tan θ))/(\\sec θ + \\tan θ) \\\\\n&= ((\\sec θ - \\tan θ)(\\sec θ + \\tan θ))/((\\sec θ + \\tan θ)) \\\\\n&= \\sec θ - \\tan θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sec\\theta + \\tan\\theta} = \\sec{\\theta} - \\tan{\\theta}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045585 false]] :edb/tempids {"e-3366" 17592186124267 "e-3368" 17592186128899 "e-3377" 17592186124268 "e-3381" 17592186124269 "e-3382" 17592186124270 "e-3432" 17592186124271 "e-3433" 17592186124272 "e-3434" 17592186124273 "e-3435" 17592186124274 "e-3449" 17592186124275 "e-345" 17592186100650 "e-3450" 17592186124276 "e-3452" 17592186119583 "e-3453" 17592186119584 "e-3454" 17592186119585 "e-3455" 17592186188207 "e-3457" 17592186091985 "e-3458" 17592186091986 "e-346" 17592186100651 "e-3460" 17592186091987 "e-3463" 17592186188208 "e-3464" 17592186091988 "e-3465" 17592186091989 "e-3466" 17592186091990 "e-3470" 17592186110459 "e-3472" 17592186110460 "e-3473" 17592186110461 "e-3479" 17592186128900 "e-3480" 17592186110462 "e-3481" 17592186128901 "e-3482" 17592186110463 "e-3483" 17592186128902 "e-3484" 17592186110464 "e-3485" 17592186110465 "e-3486" 17592186128903 "e-3497" 17592186110466 "e-3511" 17592186091991 "e-3513" 17592186091992 "e-3514" 17592186091993 "e-3515" 17592186091994 "e-3516" 17592186091995 "e-3517" 17592186091996 "e-3518" 17592186059499 "e-3519" 17592186059500 "e-3520" 17592186184058 "e-3522" 17592186059502 "e-3523" 17592186059503 "e-3524" 